WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.210
So we'll get started.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.760
Today, we're going to
cover a mixture of experts.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.570
Last year, this was a fun bonus
lecture that I threw together.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.320
But this year, thanks to
lots of people doing MoEs,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.240
this has become a much
more critical lecture.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.000
So I've added a lot of
the recent developments.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.070
And at the end, we'll try to
walk through DeepSeek-V3 and try

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.370
to understand what are all
the components that make up

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.330
a state-of-the-art
open-source system,

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.800
or at least on the architecture
side, what that looks like.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.190
So mixture of
experts is how a lot

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:41.060
of the most-modern,
high-performance systems today

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.220
are built and deployed.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.990
So there was the funny
NVIDIA leak of GPT-4

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.300
actually being potentially
revealed as GPT-MoE-1 BT.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.750
But more broadly, others like
Grok, and DeepSeek, and Llama 4

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.900
now have all adopted a mixture
of experts architecture.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.385
And it seems like at
this point, in 2025,

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:09.440
that the advantage of
mixtures of experts

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.650
over dense architectures
is very much clear.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.460
Almost all compute
scales training a mixture

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.050
of experts model,
if you do it well,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.270
is going to give you
benefits over a dense model.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.420
And so everyone
seems to be doing it

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.990
in both the East and the West.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.810
And so this will be
an important thing

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.140
to understand if you're trying
to build the best model that you

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:35.090
can for the FLOPs that you have.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.940
So mixture of experts
is very simple.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.568
It's a very terribly
named concept.

00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:42.110
I think you hear a
mixture of experts

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.540
and you think, oh, there
must be experts specialized

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.280
for different domains and
they're doing different things.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.760
There's a coding expert
and an English expert

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.280
and an other languages expert.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.440
It is very far from
that mental model.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.850
A mixture of experts is a
type of fancy architecture

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.470
that has several
subcomponents called experts

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.620
that are activated sparsely.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.440
And in particular, when you
think about mixture of experts,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.490
you should be thinking
about the MLPs.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.550
This is where all the action is.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.210
So a MoE architecture and a
non-MoE architecture are going

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.840
to be similar in almost all of
its components except for one,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.000
and that is--

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.310
if you look at this
slide over here,

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.070
this is the components of
a standard transformer.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.880
You got your self-attention
and you got your FFN.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.110
And if you zoom in,
in a dense model,

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.630
the feedforward
component just is there.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.610
It's one big block.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.800
In a sparse model,
what you would do

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.720
is that you would take this
FFN and you would split it up

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.595
or you would copy it.

00:02:40.595 --> 00:02:42.928
Depending on how you're going
to be setting up your MoE,

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:45.000
you're going to have
multiple copies, let's say,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.400
of your FFN, your fully
connected networks,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.060
and you're going to have a
router that picks some smaller

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.610
number of those in each forward
pass or at each inference.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.910
So this is the basic
idea behind the MoE.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.190
And we're going to replace
this one big feedforward

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.540
on the left side with a selector
layer and many smaller ones.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.200
And what's the
advantage of this thing?

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.320
Well, if it's sparsely
activated, that is,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.130
let's say it only
picks one expert,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.580
and an expert is the same
size as your dense FFN,

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.610
then the FLOPs between the
left side and the right side--

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.500
the dense model
and the MoE model--

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:17.780
they have the same FLOPs.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.490
They're doing the
same matrix multiplies

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.340
as you do your forward pass.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.875
So you have more parameters
without affecting your FLOPs.

00:03:25.875 --> 00:03:28.250
And if you're a believer that
what matters is having more

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.200
parameters to, for example,
memorize facts about the world,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.500
well, this is a
great architecture.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.640
So you can see the
intuition behind MoEs.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.620
Hopefully, that's
all very clear.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.410
And you might wonder,
OK, so it makes sense

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:45.360
that you can get more
parameters per FLOPs,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.440
but does that translate to
actually better performance

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.510
for the models that
you're training?

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.000
And there's been, I think, at
this point, many, many, many

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.010
papers showing that at
the same FLOP count,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.840
at the same training
amount of FLOPs,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.370
you get better performance
out of a mixture of experts

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.510
than out of a dense model.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.750
So this is a nice paper.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.540
So today, I'm going to go over
a couple of the classic Google

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.690
papers that put
this field together.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.790
And this is one of them
by Fedus et al., 2022,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.820
where they show that if you--

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:17.960
FLOPs match.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.240
You're training FLOPs,
so that's the same amount

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.830
of compute used for training.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.580
And as you increase
the number of experts,

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.247
the training loss of
your language model

00:04:25.247 --> 00:04:28.015
just keeps going down and down
and down and down and down.

00:04:28.015 --> 00:04:29.850
So more experts, better.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:31.590
Of course, the
experts aren't free.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.870
You need to store the
memory for these experts.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.328
And when you do
parallelism, you're

00:04:35.328 --> 00:04:39.140
going to have to think about
routing your data into 256

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.230
separate experts.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.330
So there's going to be
systems complexities.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.910
But if you're only
thinking about FLOPs,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.940
this is a great chart to see
because you have the same FLOPs,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.270
but you've gotten
free test loss here.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.690
And you see the same thing
reflected on the right side.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:56.310
As you train for
longer and longer,

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.290
the switch base
with 128 experts,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:03.530
the model with more experts,
gets better perplexity faster.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.750
So hopefully, that
is quite clear.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:08.250
You might say, well,
this is a 2022 paper.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:12.440
Is this true on modern
architectures, on modern scales?

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.250
It continues to
very much be true.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.290
AI2 had a very nice
paper on OlMoE,

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.010
which did a whole bunch
of ablations and carefully

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.640
controlled comparisons
into dense versus MoE

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:28.538
and other architectures, and
they see exactly the same thing.

00:05:28.538 --> 00:05:31.080
So here, on the left side-- this
is still from Fedus et al.--

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.710
you see the 7x speed up
from having many experts.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:36.450
On the right side, this
is the OlMoE comparison.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:41.180
You see, the pink one is the
MoE, and the teal one is dense.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.190
And the training loss
for the dense model

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.840
goes down much more
slowly than the MoE.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.440
So hopefully, I
have, in some sense,

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.560
sold you on the value of MoEs
for learning this slightly

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.240
new architecture.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.500
So we're going to pay a
price for all of this.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.060
But at least the FLOPs level,
this looks very compelling.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:05:59.560
Right.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.290
So yes, question.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:05.450
In the last lecture, you
mentioned, for instance, you

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:10.440
used biased archetypes,
although that is a pretty cheap

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.713
[INAUDIBLE].

00:06:12.713 --> 00:06:14.880
The facts are actually
[INAUDIBLE] [? approximate ?]

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.050
pretty badly.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:19.470
We're loading in
and out [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.610
see a serious error at runtime.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:22.110
Right.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.390
So the question was,
in last lecture,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:29.040
I was saying even small
non-FLOPs-- negligible FLOPs can

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.000
be really big in wall clock.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.280
Is anything in the MoE world
going to look like that?

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.670
And so I think one of
the drawbacks of MoE,

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:38.400
why that's not the standard
thing that's being taught,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.790
let's say, at 224n, is because
there's significant systems

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.780
complexities to making
this thing efficient.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:45.430
So I'll get to that.

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.530
It's possible to make these
things very efficient,

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.340
especially if each expert
lives on a separate device,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.460
so that you're routing
data to different places.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.020
You can be very efficient when
you do that, but it's not easy.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.920
So there's a lot of
infrastructural concerns,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.530
and you're going to see
a lot of complexities

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:01.770
to get this thing to work.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.710
But when it does
work, you're putting

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:04.810
all of your FLOPs to use.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:08.630
OK.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:12.050
And then the last one
that I wanted to show

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.340
is a lot of the companies
really love MoEs

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.090
because you get to
present plots that look

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.620
very compelling, like this.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.440
This was from the
DeepSeek-V2 paper.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.240
On the x-axis-- this is a
little bit of sleight of hand.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:25.950
This is only
activated parameters.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.670
So this is only the parameters
that are used for computation.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.070
So you ignore all the
deactivated experts.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.800
And the y-axis is
MMLU performance.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.090
And we see DeepSeek-V2.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:36.590
Wow.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.850
Look, very few
activated parameters.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.160
Really good MMLU
performance right.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.940
And so if you're only interested
in both training and inference

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.890
FLOPs, activated parameters
is the name of the game.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.510
You get really good
performance here.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.010
And this is not
just an ablation.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.730
This is a real system that
someone spent a lot of money

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:55.010
to train and deploy
it out in the wild.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:08:00.650
And we'll see this pattern
recur in other examples as well.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:02.010
Oh, was there a question?

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:02.510
Oh, no.

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:03.320
Good.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.080
All right.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.240
And so the systems thing
that is also a benefit

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.278
is that MoEs allow us to have
another axis of parallelism.

00:08:12.278 --> 00:08:14.570
So I'm going to get into
parallelism in much, much more

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.562
detail in the systems lectures.

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:19.020
I'm going to talk about how
you're going to take your model

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.290
and you're going to cut it
up into many small pieces

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.500
and lay them out across
many different devices.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.760
I'm going to talk at
a very high level.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.400
But when you have
experts, there's

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.240
a very natural way to
parallelize at the expert level.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.340
So you have multiple
different feedforward blocks.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.309
You can take each
of these experts

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.330
and you can put them
on a different device.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.950
And because experts are sparsely
activated, all you have to do

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.622
is take your token and route
it to the appropriate device,

00:08:45.622 --> 00:08:47.580
and the computation will
happen on that device.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.000
So it's a natural
cutting point to be

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.590
able to shard your model
into different devices.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.530
And so this is called
expert parallelism.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.540
And this is another reason
why MoEs are very popular.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.240
If you really want to
parallelize really big models,

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.050
this is a thing that
you're going to have to do.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:08.960
And interestingly enough, I
think MoEs developed at Google,

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:12.990
and many of the Frontier Labs,
the closed labs, were doing it.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.370
But I think the open results
actually came from China

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.900
very frequently.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:22.500
Qwen and DeepSeek were doing
a lot of MoE work last year.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.200
And it's only really
recently that I

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.090
think Western
open-source groups have

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.500
started to do more MoE work.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:30.240
So Mixtral, Grok--

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.820
I guess Grok is not open--

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.940
and then now Llama is
now an MoE architecture.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.990
And so here, Llama
4 just got released.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:39.320
Latest and greatest.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.180
This is also a sparse MoE.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.033
And I'll talk about
Llama 4 as well

00:09:43.033 --> 00:09:44.200
as I go through the lecture.

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:51.170
As I said before, so one of
the starting points for this

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:54.590
is some of the Chinese
groups, Qwen and DeepSeek,

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.780
have actually done
some really nice work

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.930
benchmarking and
understanding and evaluating

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:01.640
some of these MoE results--

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:02.540
so these MoEs.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:08.810
So Qwen1.5 was one of the first
models that I knew of to have

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:12.800
this large scale, well-tested,
well-documented MoE.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:17.120
And what they did was they took
a Qwen1.5 dense model and they

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.450
had a nice trick to upcycle
it into a mixture of experts.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:23.900
That's a clever kind of
trick, to take a dense model

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.410
and then turn it into an MoE.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:27.360
And they showed
significant gains,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.600
at least in terms of
compute efficiency,

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.230
while decreasing the total
number of parameters relative

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:36.200
to their 7B model.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:39.353
DeepSeek, which is now
famous, but originally,

00:10:39.353 --> 00:10:40.770
when these papers
were coming out,

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:44.520
were not quite as famous,
did some of the, I think,

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.210
really foundational MoE work
in the open-source world.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:50.830
A big part of this
lecture is actually

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:53.950
going to be tracing the
trajectory of the DeepSeek MoE

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:54.890
architecture.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.920
But if you look at their
original DeepSeek MoE paper,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.830
you'll see very nice papers--

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.192
sorry, very nice
comparison, showing things

00:11:02.192 --> 00:11:04.150
like, what happens when
you train a dense model

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:05.720
with a particular
amount of FLOPs?

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.410
What happens when you train
a really naive MoE that

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:09.950
doesn't do very smart routing?

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:10.640
What happens?

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.180
And then if you use a smarter
routing called the switch MoE,

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:14.722
what happens?

00:11:14.722 --> 00:11:17.055
And so you'll see all of these
very carefully controlled

00:11:17.055 --> 00:11:18.190
comparisons, and you see--

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:20.350
as you go from dense to sparse.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:22.640
So that's the leftmost column
to the rightmost column.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.550
You see all of these
benchmark metrics

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:28.310
very consistently improve
for a fixed amount of FLOPs.

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:31.270
So this is very consistent.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:35.110
And one thing that I think
almost everyone at this point

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.120
has probably heard
of is DeepSeek-V3,

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.390
and that's in some
sense a culmination

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:42.020
of all of this line of work.

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:43.900
But if you hadn't
been following MoEs

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:47.253
and you were excited about
this branch of neural networks

00:11:47.253 --> 00:11:49.420
and language modeling, you
would have actually known

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.200
about DeepSeek long
before V3 got popular.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:55.280
And we'll see at the
very end of this lecture,

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:58.780
actually, DeepSeek-V3 is not
very different from the very

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:00.310
earliest DeepSeek MoEs.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:02.830
Architecturally,
they had nailed it

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:05.950
way back when they were
training these much smaller

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:07.580
two-billion-parameter models.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.820
They really just got
the engineering right

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.430
to get something that is
actually really quite remarkably

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.810
good, which is their V3 model.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.562
OK.

00:12:18.562 --> 00:12:22.030
So now I think I have spent
quite a few minutes trying

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:23.993
to really hype you up on MoEs.

00:12:23.993 --> 00:12:25.910
And they really are, I
think, worth hyping up.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:27.340
They're very good.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:28.930
But I think there's
a question of, why

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:30.920
haven't they been more popular?

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:34.660
Why isn't it the standard thing
we teach in NLP and language

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.580
modeling classes?

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.780
It's just that they're very
complex and they're very messy.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.530
And I'm hoping that
they'll get simplified

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:45.920
over the next few years, but
they still remain pretty nasty.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:49.710
So one of the things is the
infrastructure is very complex.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.710
And the biggest
advantages of MoEs

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.210
really happen when you're
doing multi-node training.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:57.050
When you have to split up
your models anyway, then

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.090
it starts to make sense to shard
experts across different models.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.590
That's a very
natural thing to do.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:04.310
But until you get to
that point, maybe MoEs

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:05.930
are not quite as good.

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:07.995
So some of the
earlier Google papers

00:13:07.995 --> 00:13:09.620
really talk about
this trade-off, where

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.328
they say, actually, when you get
these really big models that you

00:13:12.328 --> 00:13:16.190
have to split up, then
experts become uniquely good.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.490
There's also other things
that are really tricky.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:23.090
If you think about it carefully,
this decision of which expert

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:26.002
you route tokens to is a very
difficult thing to learn.

00:13:26.002 --> 00:13:28.460
In deep learning, we really
like differentiable objectives,

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:30.530
very smooth things that
we can take gradients of.

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:32.570
Routing decisions are
not differentiable

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:35.520
because we have to pick and
commit to a particular expert.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.490
So if we're doing
that, we're going

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.330
to have a very tricky
optimization problem.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:41.290
And the training
objectives to make

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:45.230
that work is either
heuristic and/or unstable.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.980
And so we're going to have
to really carefully engineer

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.790
those guys to get them to work.

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:53.110
So those are two reasons
why you don't really

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:57.280
want to maybe do this normally.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:58.880
So what do MoEs look like?

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:03.070
As I started this lecture
with-- the classic MoEs

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.920
that you should think of is
you take the densely connected

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:08.510
layers, the FFNs,
and you split them up

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:12.130
or you copy them and you
have sparse routing decisions

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:12.920
among them.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:15.140
Of course, you could do
the same kind of idea.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.630
You could have a sparsely
rooted attention layer--

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:19.130
and some people have done this.

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:21.340
There's been a couple of
papers and a couple releases

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.530
that have taken this approach--

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.760
but it is actually quite rare
to see this in the major model

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:28.640
releases.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:31.820
I think I've seen people
talking on the internet saying,

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.600
this approach is actually
really much even more unstable

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:38.450
and very difficult to
really train consistently.

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:41.340
I haven't really seen the
ablations to back that out,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:42.860
but certainly there
haven't really

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.170
been many people training
those kinds of models

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.515
with MoE attentions.

00:14:48.515 --> 00:14:52.190
So now, I've told you about
the basic architecture.

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:53.280
It's really simple.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.580
It's just you have a router
of some kind and you route,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:57.900
and then you have
different MLPs.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.670
So what are the things that
might vary across different MoE

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:02.870
choices?

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.000
You might ask, how do we route?

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:07.920
The routing function is an
obviously important choice.

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.800
How many experts and how
big should the experts be?

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:11.925
That's another choice.

00:15:11.925 --> 00:15:13.550
And then the final
one is, how would we

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.370
train this router, this
non-differentiable objective

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:18.000
that seems very
difficult to train?

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.235
So those are very
important design questions.

00:15:20.235 --> 00:15:21.860
And we're going to
go through each one,

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:26.040
hopefully covering the design
space of all these MoE things.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:26.540
Yeah.

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:28.370
Any questions before
I get into each one

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:32.250
of these different
subcomponents here?

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:32.750
Good.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:33.250
OK.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.500
So if you're interested
in just understanding

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:43.550
a broad overview of MoEs,
at least circa 2022,

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:46.730
there's a really nice
survey or a review paper

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.010
by Fedus et al. in 2022
that covers a lot of these.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:53.695
And many of my figures are
credited to that paper.

00:15:53.695 --> 00:15:55.070
If we're thinking
about how we're

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:59.660
going to route or essentially
match tokens to experts--

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:03.320
this is the core component of
an MoE, because what a MoE does

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:04.205
is--

00:16:04.205 --> 00:16:05.603
tokens are going
to be coming in.

00:16:05.603 --> 00:16:07.520
You have your sequence
that you're processing,

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.460
and those sequences are going
to be assigned to experts.

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:12.420
Not all experts will
process every token.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.080
That's the whole point
of a sparsely routed MoE.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:18.810
And so you can ask, how are
these routing decisions made?

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:22.290
So you can have three
different kinds of choices.

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:25.190
You can have token choice,
where each token is

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.440
going to have a
routing preference

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.070
for different experts, and I
will choose the top-K experts

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:32.310
for each token.

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:35.870
Or I can have expert choice,
where each expert is going

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.297
to have a rank
preference over tokens,

00:16:38.297 --> 00:16:40.880
and then I'm going to choose the
top-K tokens for each expert.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:44.740
This has a really nice benefit
of being balanced over experts.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.780
And then the last
one is you could

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:49.390
solve some of complicated
optimization problem

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.330
to make sure that the mapping
between experts and tokens

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:53.740
is somehow balanced.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:57.520
This is global assignment.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:01.730
And just to give you a
bit of a teaser here,

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:04.810
almost all the MoEs
do token choice

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:08.050
top-K. In the
early days of MoEs,

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:10.160
people tried many,
many different things,

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:15.130
spanning this whole spectrum of
design space of token routers.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.530
If you look at the big
releases, they have all

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:21.319
converged to basically one
class of routing mechanisms,

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:23.500
which is token choice
top-K. So each token

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:26.510
is going to rank order
experts by affinity,

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:28.480
and then there's going
to be a top-K choice

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:29.950
for each one of these.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:32.702
And OlMoE, which I'll keep
referring to throughout this

00:17:32.702 --> 00:17:34.660
lecture, because they
have a really nice series

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.360
of ablations-- so it's
really nice to teach off of--

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:38.900
have exactly this ablation.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:41.530
They compare a token choice
routing versus an expert

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:42.233
choice routing.

00:17:42.233 --> 00:17:44.150
And they show, if you
look at validation loss,

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:46.600
token choice is much,
much nicer behaved,

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:49.100
much faster in loss decay.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:49.780
Yes.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:54.190
Is this a function of the
token itself or its position?

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:57.470
It's a function of
the hidden state.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:17:59.980
So the token is going to get
processed with all the position

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.157
embeddings and so on.

00:18:01.157 --> 00:18:02.740
And then the hidden
state will come in

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:04.510
and then it will be
processed by the MLP.

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:07.630
And so for the other two--

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:10.210
for the experts choosing
the token and also the

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:12.820
[? next ?] one, when you
say that it's more balanced

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:17.410
across the experts, are they--

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:20.120
it's still for the current
like token sequence,

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.570
but it's forcing them
to be more restrictive?

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:24.790
I guess, I'm saying--

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:26.780
It's still going to be
the same set of tokens.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.620
But really, it's about the
ranking selector function.

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:32.590
In token choice, I'm just
going to take the top-K

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:35.602
amongst the columns, like maybe
the scores are even identical.

00:18:35.602 --> 00:18:37.810
I'm just going to take the
top-K amongst the columns.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:42.220
In expert choice, I'm going to
take top-K amongst the rows.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:44.995
And top-K amongst
the columns is nice

00:18:44.995 --> 00:18:46.370
because you might
be able to say,

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:48.730
oh, I can define a
scoring function such

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.620
that the score is how
well each token gets

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:51.860
processed by each expert.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.830
And token choice will
route me to the best

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.190
expert for that token.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:56.690
So that makes sense
from processing.

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:58.150
But expert choice
has the benefit

00:18:58.150 --> 00:19:01.130
that each expert gets exactly
the same number of tokens.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:02.110
And so now, you might--

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:04.700
if you're putting different
experts on different devices,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:06.215
you've got balanced utilization.

00:19:06.215 --> 00:19:07.840
So there's different
trade-offs at play

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.320
as you think about routing.

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:10.870
Yes.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:14.360
How does a token know which
expert is the best for it?

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:14.860
Yeah.

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:16.930
So the question was,
how does each token

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:18.200
know which expert is good?

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.240
That is exactly the
role of the router.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:22.130
And I'll give you
the router equation.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.530
But to give you a bit of--

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:25.870
not really a spoiler,
but the routers

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:27.620
are much more lightweight
than you think.

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:30.680
So your token, let's say, is
represented by a vector x.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.470
That's your hidden
residual stream coming in.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:36.920
So now x is going to get
multiplied by w, a matrix.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.590
And then you'll just take
a sigmoid or something,

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:40.580
and that's the score.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:43.720
So it's really just a
vector-vector inner product,

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:46.750
almost like an attention
operation in a way.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:47.260
Yes.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:51.170
Is top-k the top one here, or
is it each token describes one

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:51.820
expert?

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:52.320
Right.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.450
So the choice of-- so the
question was, is K1 here?

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:57.650
So K is actually
a hyperparameter.

00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.370
And different MoEs will
choose different things.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:01.640
I will talk about this again.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.110
But to give you the high-level
intuition, the initial argument

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:06.940
that the earliest
MoE papers made

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:09.290
was that K should
be greater than 2,

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:11.415
because that way you
get some exploration.

00:20:11.415 --> 00:20:12.790
If you're doing
K equals 1, maybe

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.950
you're just always
exploiting the best arm,

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.200
and you'll never know about
the potential other things

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:17.742
you could do.

00:20:17.742 --> 00:20:20.072
But if K is 2, then
maybe that second arm

00:20:20.072 --> 00:20:22.280
can tell you a little bit
of exploration information.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:25.960
So K equals 2 was
the canonical choice.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.815
And K equals 2 actually
continues to be very popular.

00:20:28.815 --> 00:20:30.440
So that would be like
double the FLOPs.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:31.450
It's like--

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:32.240
That's right.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:32.510
That's right.

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:33.802
So that would double the FLOPs.

00:20:33.802 --> 00:20:36.340
And when people talk
about MoEs, they usually

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:38.720
say things like, x number
of activated parameters,

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.178
and that would
account for the fact

00:20:40.178 --> 00:20:42.910
that you're putting into MLPs.

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:43.550
Yes.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:48.280
So when K is greater
than 1, [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:50.650
combine the outputs of
the different experts

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:51.630
into [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:52.130
Yes.

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:54.803
So the question was when K is
1, do the outputs get combined?

00:20:54.803 --> 00:20:55.490
That's right.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:56.950
That's right.

00:20:56.950 --> 00:20:59.860
I guess look at the
attention diagram over there.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:00.980
You got the router.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:03.340
It's routed to two
MLPs up top and then

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.300
they get combined
together right after.

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:08.500
So that's exactly right.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:10.930
So in that case, you can just
[? have ?] a single average

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:12.140
and a weighted average?

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:15.290
So the question was, how
does the aggregation happen?

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:17.830
It's just the sum.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:18.440
Right.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:20.960
So I'm going to go
over the variants.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:23.440
Very common variants
that people do.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:26.770
And really, in some ways,
all you need to know

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:28.990
is top-K in order to
actually implement

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:30.880
a high-performance MoE.

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.380
But I'll give you
the other variants

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.780
because they're natural
things you might think of.

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:38.780
Top-K routing is what is used
in most MoEs, token choice

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:39.530
top-k routing.

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:42.520
So how that works is you have
your residual stream inputs

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.780
x that will go into a router.

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:46.390
And as I said, a
router is really

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:47.660
like the attention operation.

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:50.150
There's a linear inner
product and then a softmax.

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:55.640
And then you pick the top-K
most highly activated experts,

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:58.270
and then those
outputs are gated.

00:21:58.270 --> 00:21:59.770
Depending on the
implementation, you

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:03.310
might weight the outputs
based on this router weight

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:04.480
or you might not.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:07.450
And then you will just output
the weighted average or just

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:11.080
a straight sum, depending on how
your MoE implementation works.

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:14.710
And so a lot of the
MoE papers and methods

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:17.230
use top-K. Switch Transformer,
GShard, Grok, Mixtral,

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:25.420
Qwen, all the DeepSeek variants
use different top-K variants.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:27.610
Maybe a very surprising fact--

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:29.800
and this should really
make you think about what's

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.390
going on with MoEs--

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:34.225
there are a lot of results
that show that, actually, you

00:22:34.225 --> 00:22:36.037
don't even need a
smart router at all.

00:22:36.037 --> 00:22:37.870
You can actually just
use a hashing function

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:41.510
at the very bottom to map
these x's onto your experts.

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.060
And even if you're
doing hashing, so

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.100
no semantic
information at all, you

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:47.900
will still get gains
from a hashing-based MoE,

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:51.670
which is pretty wild.

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:55.160
Some of the earliest
work on MoEs, I think,

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.123
had the very smart
idea, and in many ways

00:22:57.123 --> 00:22:58.540
the right idea,
if you're thinking

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:03.550
about this top-down of using RL
to learn the routing behavior.

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:06.100
Of course, the choice
of where to route to

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:07.490
is a discrete decision.

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:10.190
And RL is great for
learning discrete decisions.

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:11.962
Why don't you use
RL to learn routing?

00:23:11.962 --> 00:23:13.420
It was used in some
of the earliest

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:15.340
work on mixture of experts.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:17.870
As far as I know, basically
no one does this now.

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:20.530
The compute cost to do
this is too prohibitive

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:22.400
and you already have
stability issues.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.430
You might not want to do that.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:28.060
There have been a couple of
papers that have explored things

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:30.820
like solving linear assignment
problems or optimal transport

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:32.260
style problems.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:33.590
They're very elegant.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:35.500
But once again, the
cost of doing this

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.970
is much higher than the
benefits that it gives you,

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:39.020
I think, in practice.

00:23:39.020 --> 00:23:40.603
And it hasn't
really been adopted.

00:23:40.603 --> 00:23:42.520
But there's a lot of
really interesting things

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:48.520
that people are doing like this
to try to improve the routing.

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.070
So now I can point at
this slide and really

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:54.700
talk through how
routing works in detail.

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:58.780
So this is the kind of top-K
routing that almost everyone has

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:01.540
converged to now.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:04.900
This is the router that's
used in DeepSeek V1 to 2.

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:09.430
Qwen and Grok do
almost exactly this.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:12.610
Instead of having a softmax
directly at the bottom here,

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:13.630
they do a--

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:18.200
DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral, DBRX don't
have a softmax at the bottom,

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:19.990
but they'll softmax
the g of i,t.

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:21.950
But it's a very
minor difference.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:24.760
So let's walk through
what's going on here

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:28.820
and try to reason about
the behavior of this.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:32.510
So what's happening here
is, at the very bottom,

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:33.680
we've got our inputs.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:37.070
This is our u of l input.

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:41.260
And I would like to take
this residual stream input

00:24:41.260 --> 00:24:44.403
and process it through my MoE.

00:24:44.403 --> 00:24:45.820
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:47.530
is I have to figure
out which experts

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:48.678
are going to be activated.

00:24:48.678 --> 00:24:49.970
Now, how am I going to do that?

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:53.150
Well, how I'm going to do that
is very similar to attention.

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.120
I'm going to take my u, which
is my residual stream input,

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:59.300
and I'm going to take the
inner products with the e of i.

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:03.820
These are learned vectors
that are for each expert that

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:07.425
tells the expert, I'm an expert
that points in this direction.

00:25:07.425 --> 00:25:09.760
And so I'm computing,
in this inner product

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.900
here, expert and input affinity.

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:16.570
And I'm computing a softmax to
determine for each token, what

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:18.260
are the best experts?

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:19.030
So I normalize.

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:20.620
This is s of i of t.

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:24.800
Now, I take the s of i of t and
I go through a top-K function.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:30.830
I only select the K best weights
and then I use this as my gate.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.520
So I zero out
everything else and I

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:38.020
take the weighted average of
each of the expert's outputs.

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:41.690
And then I add that to my
original residual stream,

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:43.550
and then I return that.

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:47.740
So this is hopefully very
familiar to what you're all

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:51.560
very familiar with in terms
of how a transformer works,

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:54.920
with only the difference of
this top-K routing piece.

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:59.630
Is that clear to everyone
how this thing works?

00:25:59.630 --> 00:26:00.130
Good.

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:01.180
Excellent.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:04.450
So in some sense, the mechanics
of the forward process

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:06.340
of the routing is very simple.

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:09.880
What is mystifying
is that fact that you

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.000
can learn this very well.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:13.360
This is, in some sense,
a fairly complicated set

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:15.950
of things to have to learn
to do well by a model.

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:17.960
Yes.

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:22.210
So we're using softmax here.

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:25.480
Previously, you talked about
the benefits of softmax,

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:29.140
is that it's going to push you
pretty extremely to choosing

00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:30.740
a singular max.

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:33.280
It's not a hardmax,
but it [INAUDIBLE]--

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:36.670
so I'm having trouble thinking
of the intuition of putting

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:41.740
softmax basically on top of
combining it with the top-K,

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:44.500
where you're getting multiple,
and then you're using something

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:48.440
that's going to push me towards
choosing just one thing.

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:48.940
Yeah.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:54.766
I mean, I think maybe one way of
thinking about the softmax is--

00:26:54.766 --> 00:26:57.190
the whole purpose of
this is just to make it

00:26:57.190 --> 00:27:00.890
so that when I average my
experts later, it sums to 1.

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:04.070
Don't think of the softmax
as a softmax operation,

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:05.920
even though that's
literally the name.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:09.650
Really, the softmax operation
is a normalize-to-1 operation.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:11.650
And the normalize-to-1
operation is

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.170
going to make that a
weighted average up top.

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:15.790
The other thing
that's very important

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:19.100
is, you might think, why can't
I just get rid of the top-K?

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.800
Why don't I just
use the softmax here

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:22.910
and just gate all the experts?

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:26.260
Well, then you immediately lose
the systems efficiency aspect

00:27:26.260 --> 00:27:26.980
of this.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:29.630
You have to have
top-K during training.

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:32.530
Otherwise, you pay the
training cost of all capital

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:33.920
N of your experts.

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:36.120
This is the key
thing about MoEs.

00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:38.470
We have to do all
of this gymnastics

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.140
to make sure that both
that training time

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:43.500
and inference time, we have
a sparse number of activated

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:44.000
experts.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.500
That's why we go
through the top-K. OK.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:47.000
Yes.

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:47.930
From the back.

00:27:47.930 --> 00:27:48.430
Yeah.

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:51.280
So because you're
doing softmax first

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:53.180
and then the top-K
get the weights,

00:27:53.180 --> 00:27:56.360
you no longer have the
guarantees for sum to 1, do you?

00:27:56.360 --> 00:27:58.090
So isn't this--

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:01.720
So yeah, the question
was, if you softmax first,

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:02.960
you no longer sum to 1.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:04.940
And yes, that's
absolutely right.

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.230
You no longer sum to 1.

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:08.920
And in some ways,
there's no requirement

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.020
that you have to sum to
1, because the next layer

00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:12.620
can magnify it back up.

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.210
There's layer norms everywhere.

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:15.920
It's not as if it
has to sum to 1.

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:17.337
But I think that
is the reason why

00:28:17.337 --> 00:28:19.253
some of the other
architectures basically move

00:28:19.253 --> 00:28:20.510
the location of the softmax.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:22.635
There's a aesthetic choice
about whether you really

00:28:22.635 --> 00:28:26.470
want that weight to be
normalized to 1 or not.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:27.280
Yes.

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:27.860
Yeah.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:30.400
So I was wondering, how
does the e vector here

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:33.530
relate to the weight matrices
of the feedforward network?

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:34.030
OK.

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:35.950
So the question
was whether and how

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:37.802
the e vectors relate
to the feedforward.

00:28:37.802 --> 00:28:39.260
They're not really
tied in any way.

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:40.930
The e vectors are
just learned vectors

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:43.970
for the-- just think of the e's
as parameters for the router.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:47.690
There's a separate
objects from the FFN.

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:48.190
Yeah.

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:48.690
Yeah.

00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:51.460
I was just wondering,
how does this compare

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:54.550
to sampling from the softmax?

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:55.150
Great.

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:56.980
The question was about,
how does it compare

00:28:56.980 --> 00:28:58.720
to sampling from the softmax?

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:00.560
You can sample from the softmax.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:05.590
And some methods actually
do a kind of soft sampling

00:29:05.590 --> 00:29:06.370
from the softmax.

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:09.130
Specifically, one
of the Google papers

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:12.380
has a procedure where they take
the top element of the softmax

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:14.980
and then they randomly sample
the second element proportional

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:17.440
to the remainder of the softmax.

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:20.090
And that gives you more
exploration, which is good.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:21.790
But the drawback of
that is that if you

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:23.332
don't sample at test
time, now you've

00:29:23.332 --> 00:29:26.630
got to train test mismatch.

00:29:26.630 --> 00:29:27.130
OK.

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:28.210
Yes.

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:31.330
Can I just renormalize
after top-K?

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.650
Why not just renormalize
after top-K was the question?

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:34.750
Is that right?

00:29:34.750 --> 00:29:35.780
Some models do.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.020
Some models do renormalize
after the top-K,

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:39.200
but that's kind of a choice.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.300
Some architectures
don't do that.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:42.290
Some architectures do.

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.860
It doesn't actually matter
because the scale can

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:46.580
be basically adjusted post hoc.

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:50.110
So there's no reason why it
has to sum to 1 after the g

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:53.020
operation.

00:29:53.020 --> 00:29:53.980
Cool.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:54.770
Oh, sorry.

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:55.520
Yes.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:58.690
So bias-- [INAUDIBLE] term--

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:01.350
is you [INAUDIBLE] up there.

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:01.850
Yes.

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:03.940
So it first
[? turned ?] into sum,

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:08.800
if g is approximating
a probability vector

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:13.120
could be seen as an expectation
of the function FFN?

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:14.020
Right.

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:15.550
Plus u--

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:18.680
So FFN actually-- this is
not an expectation of FFN,

00:30:18.680 --> 00:30:21.640
because each FFN
is a different FFN.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:24.080
So this is not actually
an expectation.

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:26.050
And the gates are sparse.

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:28.900
So this is like a weighted
selection operation over K

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:31.320
different-- or actually,
capital N different FFNs.

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:31.820
OK.

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:33.730
And then the [? utl ?]
at the very end

00:30:33.730 --> 00:30:35.650
there, if you remember
the transformer, that's

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:36.770
the residual stream.

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:39.483
So I'm adding back the inputs
because I want an identity

00:30:39.483 --> 00:30:40.400
connection throughout.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.310
OK.

00:30:42.310 --> 00:30:42.910
OK.

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:44.800
Oh, there's another question.

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:47.990
Why does the router have such
a basic parameterization?

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:52.390
What happens if you put more
weights into your router

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:52.940
function?

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:53.440
Great.

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:55.452
The question was, why
is the router so basic?

00:30:55.452 --> 00:30:57.410
It seems like, if you're
going to have experts,

00:30:57.410 --> 00:30:59.740
it seems important to
route to the right experts.

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:01.480
So why don't you do that?

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:05.710
I think there have been some
ablations in some of the earlier

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:09.490
Google papers on having
MLP routers and more

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:11.530
sophisticated things.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:15.390
I think the complex answer
here is that the systems

00:31:15.390 --> 00:31:17.250
concerns weigh heavily.

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:20.310
If you're using a lot of FLOPs
to make routing decisions,

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:21.730
you have to pay for those FLOPs.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:23.688
And so you have to get
performance improvements

00:31:23.688 --> 00:31:25.050
in just the routing.

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:27.750
And I think that one other
thing to appreciate here

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:30.240
is that there are really
big limits to how well you

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:33.150
can route, because the learning
process for this routing thing

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:36.120
is actually pretty dicey,
because how are you

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:39.160
going to get gradients for
which routers are good or bad?

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:41.380
Well, the only thing you
have is if you have top two,

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:43.600
then you can compare the
two things that you have,

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:45.510
and you can push the
gradients into s of t

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:47.200
because your g is a weight.

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:50.440
And then that s of t might
inform your inner product.

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:53.065
But that's a very indirect way
to be learning your affinity.

00:31:53.065 --> 00:31:54.690
So even if you make
it complex, there's

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.607
no guarantee that you're
going to really learn

00:31:56.607 --> 00:31:58.580
the optimal router.

00:31:58.580 --> 00:31:59.470
Great.

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:01.300
OK.

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:07.665
So I think one of the great
innovations of the DeepSeek MoE,

00:32:07.665 --> 00:32:12.480
which was very quickly adopted
by all the other Chinese MoE

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:16.980
releases, is this idea
of both a shared expert

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:19.530
and a fine-grained expert.

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:24.390
And so the basic MoE structure
that was originally proposed

00:32:24.390 --> 00:32:26.430
is to take your
dense architecture

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.480
and copy the experts over.

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:30.810
So in this case,
you're going to have--

00:32:30.810 --> 00:32:33.810
let's say if you
have top two routing,

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:36.000
you're going to have twice
the activated parameters

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:37.500
of your original dense model.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:39.960
So you take your MoE
and you copy it over

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.830
and you activate K equals 2.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.310
So this is what you might
think of as the vanilla

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:47.790
or the basic MoE that
you might start with.

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:52.440
People realized fairly quickly
that having lots of experts

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:53.200
is good.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:57.330
And the logical next step beyond
having lots of experts is good

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:59.160
is, I want lots
of experts, but I

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.160
don't want to pay the parameter
cost for having lots of experts.

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:06.300
And so DeepSeek basically argued
that the right thing to do then

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:09.510
was to cut the expert
up into smaller pieces.

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:12.220
So remember, last lecture,
I was telling you about,

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:14.430
oh, the golden
rule, in some sense,

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:17.635
is to have your hidden layer,
and then you multiply that by 4,

00:33:17.635 --> 00:33:19.510
and that will give you
your projection layer.

00:33:19.510 --> 00:33:21.480
So now, what you
would do is, instead

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:24.340
of multiplying by let's say
4, you might multiply by 2.

00:33:24.340 --> 00:33:26.002
So now you have
smaller matrices.

00:33:26.002 --> 00:33:27.460
You have more
fine-grained experts.

00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:29.640
You can have twice
as many of them.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:32.110
And you can take that logic
much more to the extreme.

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:34.510
You can quadruple
or multiply by 8,

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:37.620
and you can keep decreasing
the size of your projection

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:39.270
dimension there.

00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:40.570
That's fine-grained experts.

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.270
And there's drawbacks
I'll talk about later.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:44.470
It doesn't come for free.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.760
So you to be very careful about
how you structure these things.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:52.770
And then the other thing that
has been studied and noted

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:57.480
is maybe it's helpful to
have at least some MLP that

00:33:57.480 --> 00:33:59.460
can capture shared structure.

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:01.080
Maybe there's just
processing that

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:04.180
always needs to happen no matter
which token you're processing.

00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:06.450
In that case, it
seems kind of a waste

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:10.110
to do all this routing work and
to have all these parameters

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:11.800
spread out everywhere
when we can just

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:15.489
have one or few shared
experts whose job it

00:34:15.489 --> 00:34:19.040
is to handle all of this shared
processing that's needed.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.250
And so there's shared experts.

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:24.730
And so this setup of using
fine-grained experts plus shared

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:29.563
experts originally came
out in DeepSeek MoE,

00:34:29.563 --> 00:34:31.480
although I think the
original inspiration came

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:33.730
from DeepSpeed MoE.

00:34:33.730 --> 00:34:36.080
And Qwen and others--

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:40.300
so almost all of the
open MoE releases

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:44.120
since DeepSeek have adopted
some sets of these innovations,

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:47.530
because it's quite clear that
especially fine-grained experts

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:49.219
is just really, really useful.

00:34:49.219 --> 00:34:54.070
That's a kind of no-brainer
at this point to do.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:58.030
One of the things I really like
about reading DeepSeek papers

00:34:58.030 --> 00:34:59.690
is that they do ablations.

00:34:59.690 --> 00:35:03.130
It's not like a whatever
sales tech report.

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:06.130
They actually care about whether
or not their methods work.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:09.460
And so they have this lovely
ablation in the DeepSeek MoE

00:35:09.460 --> 00:35:11.265
paper, where they show--

00:35:11.265 --> 00:35:14.190
the blue bar over
here, this is GShard.

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.490
This is a very basic vanilla
implementation of an MoE.

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:20.040
You can have one
shared expert-- that's

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.590
the orange bar-- and that gives
you a big boost on some tasks

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:24.330
and no boost on others.

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:26.320
You can have
fine-grained experts.

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:28.258
That's the green
and orange bars,

00:35:28.258 --> 00:35:29.800
and you get further
boosts from that.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:31.592
And if you compare the
blue to the orange--

00:35:31.592 --> 00:35:33.120
composing all of
these differences

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:37.230
give you quite the
big boost over others.

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:39.720
And so we can see that more
experts in shared experts

00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:42.190
generally seem to help.

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:42.690
OK.

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:43.190
Yes.

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:43.770
Question.

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:47.020
In that part, when it
says 7 out of something,

00:35:47.020 --> 00:35:49.030
does that mean it's
doing like top seven?

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:49.530
Yes.

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:50.030
Sorry.

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:51.238
I should have explained that.

00:35:51.238 --> 00:35:52.020
That's right.

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:57.128
So x out of y means x activated
out of y total routed experts.

00:35:57.128 --> 00:35:57.670
That's right.

00:35:57.670 --> 00:35:58.170
Yeah.

00:35:58.170 --> 00:35:59.820
And so you can see
the pattern here

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.100
as well of, as you increase
the number of experts,

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:05.580
you also often increase the
number of activated experts,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:08.010
especially if you're doing
fine-grained experts.

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:11.220
[INAUDIBLE] wise, it's free,
because each expert is now

00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:13.270
smaller.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:13.770
Good.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:14.270
OK.

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:20.070
So OlMoE has basically
corroborating

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:23.950
evidence that shows really
nicely that these things work.

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:26.040
So the bottom
[? one-- ?] I think

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:28.200
I'll start with because
it's more decisive--

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:31.200
shows fine-grained
experts, going from 8 to 32

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:33.960
to 64 fine-grained experts,
mirroring, in some sense,

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:35.610
the DeepSeek ablations.

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:38.430
And you see very clear trends
and losses and other kinds

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:41.490
of metrics that you
see improvements

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:43.180
going from 8 to 32 to 64.

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:45.210
Fine-grained expert is great.

00:36:45.210 --> 00:36:49.740
Shared experts, which is purple
versus teal at the very top,

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.990
you actually don't see
really any gains, at least

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:53.490
in the OlMoE setup.

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:56.645
So they actually end up
going with no shared experts,

00:36:56.645 --> 00:36:59.020
even though the DeepSeek paper
seemed to show more gains.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:00.728
So that one actually
is maybe more mixed,

00:37:00.728 --> 00:37:04.410
given this follow-up or
this third-party replication

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.800
of these kinds of ideas.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:10.860
So at this point, you
might be wondering,

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:12.550
what are common configurations?

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:14.250
I think I'm going
to take the page out

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:17.970
of last lecture's
playbook of looking

00:37:17.970 --> 00:37:21.240
at a lot of the recent releases,
looking at what people do,

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:23.880
and trying to talk a little
bit about the patterns that

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:25.440
have arisen.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:29.640
So some of the early
Google papers-- so GShard,

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:32.940
Switch Transformer,
ST-MoE, some of them

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:35.737
had really large numbers
of routed experts.

00:37:35.737 --> 00:37:38.070
And there was a lot of really
interesting stuff going on

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:38.830
in those papers.

00:37:38.830 --> 00:37:40.200
I'd encourage you to read them.

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.270
Some of them happened
in LSTMs and other kinds

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:44.160
of architectures.

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.350
Regardless, very
quickly, I think

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:49.530
there was a period
of 8 to 16 experts,

00:37:49.530 --> 00:37:53.860
like Mixtral, DBRX, Grok,
with two active experts.

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:55.510
Those worked reasonably well.

00:37:55.510 --> 00:37:59.100
But then DeepSeek MoE
or DeepSeek MoE V1

00:37:59.100 --> 00:38:02.790
comes out that has the
prototypical configuration I

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:05.380
told you about-- fine-grained
experts, 64 of them,

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:08.700
six actively rooted,
two shared experts,

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:11.370
and each expert is
one-fourth the size

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.218
of a normally sized expert.

00:38:13.218 --> 00:38:15.010
Take that last column
with a grain of salt,

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:17.185
because I had to back
them out from config

00:38:17.185 --> 00:38:18.310
files and things like that.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:21.900
So I'm not 100% sure about
the exact ratios here.

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:27.730
So we've then got essentially
Qwen1.5, DeepSeek-V3, MiniMax.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:29.410
These are Chinese MoEs.

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:32.632
They follow essentially in the
same footsteps as DeepSeek-V1.

00:38:32.632 --> 00:38:34.090
The specific numbers
are different.

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:38.290
But in the sense that they
use fine-grained experts,

00:38:38.290 --> 00:38:40.300
and they often have
shared experts,

00:38:40.300 --> 00:38:43.920
they're very similar to
this original DeepSeek MoE

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:45.180
configuration.

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:48.820
OlMoE MiniMax and Llama
are very recent MoEs.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:52.980
They definitely do all this
fine-grained expert stuff.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:56.010
And Llama 4 also
uses a shared expert.

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:59.260
And you see variations
in configuration,

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:00.760
but you see what's
basically shared,

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:02.770
which is this, this
fine-grained experts idea.

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:04.187
And especially for
the big models,

00:39:04.187 --> 00:39:06.360
like Llama 4 and DeepSeek,
very, very large numbers

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:07.560
of routed experts--

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:09.970
or sorry, not routed,
total experts.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:10.570
Yes.

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:14.050
So can you re-explain what
the ratios are together?

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:17.070
The ratio is
representing roughly

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:20.850
how much each expert is
sliced relative to having

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:22.650
just the standard
dense configuration.

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:24.620
So in terms of
hyperparameters, you

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:26.620
know that if you're
following the rule of thumb,

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:30.060
your hidden dimension and
your projection in your MLP

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:32.640
should be about 1
to 4, or 1 to 2.6

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:34.120
if you're doing a gated network.

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:37.150
And so by looking at the hidden
layers of these architectures,

00:39:37.150 --> 00:39:39.795
you can see how many
times they sliced up

00:39:39.795 --> 00:39:42.970
that original feedforward size.

00:39:42.970 --> 00:39:46.870
So if, [INAUDIBLE]
about ratios 1/4,

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.000
but then they have [? six ?]
more of those experts,

00:39:49.000 --> 00:39:51.930
does that mean that's still
increasing their [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:54.188
parameters by a
factor of [? eight? ?]

00:39:54.188 --> 00:39:54.730
That's right.

00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:55.230
Yeah.

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:58.080
So you can think of
this as roughly they

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:00.960
have 16 normally sized experts.

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:03.090
And so they're, of course,
having more parameters

00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:04.810
than the density equivalent.

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:07.450
They have six routed--

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:10.150
so they have eight total
active experts at any time,

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:11.560
each that are quarter-sized.

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:14.370
And so you should think of them
as roughly double the FLOPs

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:15.700
of a dense equivalent.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:18.690
So some arithmetic,
but hopefully the math

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:20.160
is clear and
consistent, hopefully.

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:21.562
Yes.

00:40:21.562 --> 00:40:26.340
If the ratio is [? 1/4 ?]
are you projecting down?

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:30.210
Is that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:31.660
So for some of
the exotic ratios,

00:40:31.660 --> 00:40:33.670
I'm not quite sure
why they're that way,

00:40:33.670 --> 00:40:35.800
but they are very
precisely whole numbers

00:40:35.800 --> 00:40:39.090
when you take the ratios
between the FFNs and the implied

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:40.000
hyperparameters.

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:42.480
And so I think those
are exactly the split

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:44.140
counts of how much
they were sliced,

00:40:44.140 --> 00:40:48.390
but I'm not sure why
they have 1 over 14.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:51.210
Do you ever project towards
the smaller dimension

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:54.170
because the ratio is so
small, [? in ?] the MLP?

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:54.670
So yeah.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:55.320
So yeah.

00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:57.310
That's why you're asking, do
they do they down-project?

00:40:57.310 --> 00:40:57.730
Yeah, that's right.

00:40:57.730 --> 00:40:59.070
And some of them, they
are actually smaller.

00:40:59.070 --> 00:40:59.490
Yeah.

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:01.270
I don't remember which
models in particular.

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:03.478
But in some of them, I do
remember they were actually

00:41:03.478 --> 00:41:05.250
down-projected.

00:41:05.250 --> 00:41:06.125
Yes.

00:41:06.125 --> 00:41:07.500
What is the
intuition for wanting

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:09.930
more than one shared expert?

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:10.530
Yeah.

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:14.220
I mean, it does seem like
there was a period where

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:15.720
some of the Chinese
LLM companies

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:17.820
tried many shared
experts, and then

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:19.870
people have come back to 0 or 1.

00:41:19.870 --> 00:41:22.497
And if you look at
the OlMoE ablations,

00:41:22.497 --> 00:41:25.080
it's not quite clear that even
one shared expert is decisively

00:41:25.080 --> 00:41:26.220
useful.

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:29.220
I think the original
motivation was that then you

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.220
have equally sized
experts, like these

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:34.240
are both quarter-sized experts.

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:37.260
And now you have eight
active experts total,

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.310
and so you can keep
the sizes consistent.

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:42.360
Otherwise, I don't really see
a particular justification

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:45.125
for why it should be too small
or one versus one larger one.

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:48.580
OK.

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:50.910
Cool.

00:41:50.910 --> 00:41:53.040
So then, hopefully,
you get a sense

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:56.370
of how the routing works
for a lot of these MoEs

00:41:56.370 --> 00:41:57.420
and how it's all set up.

00:41:57.420 --> 00:42:00.360
The forward pass, hopefully,
you fully understand.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.050
Now we need to think
about training,

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:04.710
and training is pretty gnarly.

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:07.440
And the major challenge
I foreshadowed earlier,

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:10.620
when we train, we cannot
turn on all the experts,

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:15.180
because if we do that, then we
pay the full FLOPs cost of all

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:15.990
of the experts.

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:17.790
Having a model
that's, I don't know,

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:20.590
256 times more expensive
to train is a total no go.

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:25.110
So we need train time sparsity,
but sparse gating decisions

00:42:25.110 --> 00:42:27.010
are obviously not
differentiable.

00:42:27.010 --> 00:42:30.310
We now have a annoying
RL-ish problem.

00:42:30.310 --> 00:42:32.310
And so we could do
any of these things,

00:42:32.310 --> 00:42:34.510
like RL to optimize
gating policies.

00:42:34.510 --> 00:42:38.205
We could do bandit-inspired
things of doing randomization

00:42:38.205 --> 00:42:40.020
to do exploration.

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:42.390
Or we can just have
some heuristics

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:45.660
that try to balance things out,
put some loss terms in there,

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:47.850
and hope things work out.

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:50.190
Having gone through
deep-learning classes

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:52.620
of many kinds, you can
guess, internally, which

00:42:52.620 --> 00:42:54.510
one people use in practice.

00:42:54.510 --> 00:42:59.490
And I'll talk about each
one of these three in turn.

00:42:59.490 --> 00:42:59.990
OK.

00:42:59.990 --> 00:43:03.420
So RL, I think, is one
of the earliest things

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:04.300
that people tried.

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:06.480
It's probably the
most principled thing

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:07.740
that you can do in this space.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:11.320
You have non-differentiable
routing decision.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:14.550
Well, think of that as a
policy, throw our RL at it,

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:16.890
and then solve the problem.

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:21.060
Unfortunately, it's not better
than a lot of the other things

00:43:21.060 --> 00:43:22.530
that you can do.

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:25.050
There is a paper
by Clark, et al.

00:43:25.050 --> 00:43:28.410
in 2020, who were exploring
various scaling-related

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:31.450
questions in MoEs.

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:35.580
And they do have an RL baseline
that I was able to dig up.

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:37.560
But unfortunately,
it's not really

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:41.080
that much better than, say,
using hashing for decisions.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:43.740
And they were really interested
in benchmarking this thing

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:46.150
on the left called S-BASE,
which is a linear assignment

00:43:46.150 --> 00:43:50.530
kind of a method, and that
thing handily beats doing RL.

00:43:50.530 --> 00:43:54.000
And I think, in practice,
the gradient variances

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:56.970
and complexity means that
it's pretty finicky to use.

00:43:56.970 --> 00:44:02.100
And no one at scale has really
used an RL-based approach

00:44:02.100 --> 00:44:06.810
to optimize these gating
decisions as far as I know.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:10.020
A thing that has been
done much more at scale

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:14.190
is stochastic approximations
of various kinds.

00:44:14.190 --> 00:44:17.280
So what they might
do is they might

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:19.900
add a bit of perturbations.

00:44:19.900 --> 00:44:24.630
So here is an example of
one from Shazeer in 2017--

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:27.480
this is one of the
early MoE papers--

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:30.488
where they're still going
to do top-K routing.

00:44:30.488 --> 00:44:32.280
So they're going to
keep the top-K elements

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:35.100
of this H of x
operation and they're

00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:37.210
going to softmax
that to get the gate.

00:44:37.210 --> 00:44:41.130
But what we're going to do
to get this H of x operation

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:42.550
is the following.

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:44.100
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:47.410
going to have our
original linear affinity.

00:44:47.410 --> 00:44:49.870
This is identical to what
we were doing before.

00:44:49.870 --> 00:44:52.620
We were basically just
computing our inputs x

00:44:52.620 --> 00:44:55.660
and a learned weight
for each gate.

00:44:55.660 --> 00:44:57.100
And so this part is the same.

00:44:57.100 --> 00:44:59.410
But I'm actually now going
to jitter it a little bit.

00:44:59.410 --> 00:45:00.790
I'm going to add a normal.

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:06.400
And then I'm going to pick a
W noise scale that's learned,

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:08.520
and this thing is going
to control how much noise

00:45:08.520 --> 00:45:10.630
to inject into this process.

00:45:10.630 --> 00:45:14.020
And you can think of this as a
stochastic exploration policy.

00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:17.060
And by manipulating W
noise in particular ways,

00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:19.210
annealing it down or
doing various things,

00:45:19.210 --> 00:45:21.970
I can control the
exploration-exploitation

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:24.610
trade-offs that this
MoE is going to have.

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:27.645
And so this is going to
give you one solution

00:45:27.645 --> 00:45:30.210
to the explore-exploit dilemma.

00:45:30.210 --> 00:45:32.710
And especially if you're
noising things up,

00:45:32.710 --> 00:45:35.910
each expert might randomly
get some other tokens

00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:37.330
that it wasn't expecting to get.

00:45:37.330 --> 00:45:39.960
So it will lead to experts that
are less specialized, but maybe

00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:42.300
a little bit more robust.

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:45.630
And so that seems
generally quite nice.

00:45:45.630 --> 00:45:47.400
Of course, the
stochasticity also

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:50.020
means that you don't get
as much specialization,

00:45:50.020 --> 00:45:53.880
and that leads to
loss of efficiency.

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:56.580
And there's another
approach that people

00:45:56.580 --> 00:46:00.570
have done where they
multiply the router logits--

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:04.140
or sorry, they have a
multiplicative perturbation

00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:07.620
to the router
logits with the goal

00:46:07.620 --> 00:46:09.280
of getting less brittle experts.

00:46:09.280 --> 00:46:12.420
But this jitter
process was removed

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:14.940
in some of the later papers
because they found it just

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:17.880
didn't work as well as some
of the heuristic loss-based

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:18.610
approaches.

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:21.120
And so this was an approach
that was tried in a couple--

00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:22.980
this kind of stochastic
routing tricks

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:26.430
were tried in a couple of
the early Google papers.

00:46:26.430 --> 00:46:28.620
But I think that
has generally been

00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:32.910
abandoned by a lot of the
people training these models.

00:46:32.910 --> 00:46:34.440
OK.

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:35.580
Yes.

00:46:35.580 --> 00:46:38.938
For the stochastic, what
problem does that solve?

00:46:38.938 --> 00:46:40.480
Because we're still
taking the top-K,

00:46:40.480 --> 00:46:42.750
so we still can't
differentiate [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:42.750 --> 00:46:44.940
Well, if you think of
this-- so the question was,

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:47.470
we still can't differentiate
because we're taking the top-K.

00:46:47.470 --> 00:46:50.280
But if you change
your interpretation

00:46:50.280 --> 00:46:52.530
of the problem a
little bit, if you

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:55.170
think about a bandit problem,
it has the same structure

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:57.233
as this, where you
pull a bandit arm

00:46:57.233 --> 00:46:58.900
and you don't see any
of the other arms.

00:46:58.900 --> 00:47:01.690
So you can't really allocate
your resources efficiently.

00:47:01.690 --> 00:47:04.210
If you pull some of the
other ones at random,

00:47:04.210 --> 00:47:07.090
now you've got enough data to
be able to do some optimization.

00:47:07.090 --> 00:47:09.780
And so this jittering is
very similar in spirit

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:12.780
to this kind of
epsilon-greedy-style exploration

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:15.420
thing, where you're randomly
pulling some of the other arms

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:17.400
with some probability,
where the probability

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:20.160
itself depends on
how confident you are

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:21.640
about this routing decision.

00:47:21.640 --> 00:47:23.440
So that's kind of the intuition.

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:26.160
And then, of
course, that's going

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:29.700
to give you some way of
getting some signal back.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:31.800
OK.

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:37.680
So the thing that, in practice,
people have ended up with

00:47:37.680 --> 00:47:40.240
is, we don't do any of that.

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:41.650
We don't do RL.

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:43.920
We don't do stochastic
exploration.

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:46.500
But we rely on really
another mechanism

00:47:46.500 --> 00:47:48.760
to keep things reasonable.

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:51.700
So if we're doing top two
routing, technically speaking,

00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:54.160
we do get some signal in the
gradient descent process,

00:47:54.160 --> 00:47:56.230
because we can
compare the top two

00:47:56.230 --> 00:47:58.600
experts that we did evaluate.

00:47:58.600 --> 00:48:01.460
And so it's possible to
do some optimization.

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:03.440
But when we do ignore--

00:48:03.440 --> 00:48:06.070
if we drop all the
other constraints,

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:08.440
the big issue that
arises is you just

00:48:08.440 --> 00:48:10.887
end up picking one
expert all the time.

00:48:10.887 --> 00:48:12.470
And that expert is
good at everything,

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:14.095
and all the other
experts are terrible.

00:48:14.095 --> 00:48:15.760
You end up in this
local minimum where

00:48:15.760 --> 00:48:18.740
you've routed all of your tokens
to one expert all the time.

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:21.370
So really, the key
game becomes then,

00:48:21.370 --> 00:48:23.470
how do we get out of
that local minimum?

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:26.890
And loss balancing
or balancing losses

00:48:26.890 --> 00:48:29.270
is really the key trick
to get out of this.

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:31.450
And this is kind of
important to understand

00:48:31.450 --> 00:48:34.210
because this is the loss
that mostly everyone actually

00:48:34.210 --> 00:48:36.580
uses to train the models.

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:38.743
So if you were
zoning out earlier,

00:48:38.743 --> 00:48:40.660
you probably should make
sure to pay attention

00:48:40.660 --> 00:48:43.840
to this particular
set of equations here.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:47.470
So this is originally from
the Switch Transformer,

00:48:47.470 --> 00:48:49.870
from Fedus et al., in 2022.

00:48:49.870 --> 00:48:51.810
And they add this
particular loss,

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:53.520
where what they're
going to do is

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:56.470
they're going to loop all
over each of the experts,

00:48:56.470 --> 00:48:58.600
and they're going to take
the-- you could think

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:00.600
of this as an inner product
between the vector f

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:02.290
and the vector P.

00:49:02.290 --> 00:49:03.640
And so what are these vectors?

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:07.260
Well, f is-- for each
of the experts, this

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:10.870
is the fraction of the tokens
that were allocated to expert i.

00:49:10.870 --> 00:49:13.680
So you can think of this as
a probability vector that's

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:16.530
telling me what fraction
of my tokens in my batch

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:22.740
or in my whatever the unit is
here, did I route to expert i?

00:49:22.740 --> 00:49:27.600
Now, P of i is the fraction
of the router probability

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:29.620
that was allocated to expert i.

00:49:29.620 --> 00:49:35.250
So the router probability
is the original softmax

00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:38.230
routing decision that I
was intending to send.

00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:39.690
So this is measuring--

00:49:39.690 --> 00:49:43.990
P of i is what was the intended
probability from the router.

00:49:43.990 --> 00:49:50.490
And then f of i what was the
actual routing decision made

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:52.840
by the top-K method.

00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:56.100
And one thing that's kind of
interesting to look at here

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:58.590
is let's say we take the
derivative of that loss

00:49:58.590 --> 00:50:00.820
with respect to P of i.

00:50:00.820 --> 00:50:05.260
So this is the linear function
with respect to P of i.

00:50:05.260 --> 00:50:09.120
And you'll see that the
strongest down-weighting action

00:50:09.120 --> 00:50:13.120
happens on the biggest experts
with the biggest allocations.

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:16.410
So it's actually, in fact,
proportional to the amount

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:18.130
of tokens that you get.

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:23.160
So you're going to be pushed
downwards more strongly if you

00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:24.520
got more tokens.

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:27.640
And so this is the basic
behavior of this loss.

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:30.720
And almost everybody
uses this kind of f

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:34.740
dot P kind of a trick
to try to balance tokens

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:35.950
across different units.

00:50:35.950 --> 00:50:39.330
So the basic unit that you might
want to balance over initially

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:40.000
is batches.

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:42.850
You might want each batch to
get allocated evenly to experts,

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:47.310
but you might actually have
other kinds of balancing

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:48.670
that you might want to do.

00:50:48.670 --> 00:50:50.867
And DeepSeek does exactly
this kind of thing.

00:50:50.867 --> 00:50:53.200
I'll talk about all the
variants that they've thrown in,

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:56.140
but the first thing is
per-expert balancing per batch.

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:58.470
So each batch, they want
to make sure experts get

00:50:58.470 --> 00:50:59.710
an even number of tokens.

00:50:59.710 --> 00:51:01.840
And this is from
the DeepSeek paper.

00:51:01.840 --> 00:51:04.270
And hopefully, this looks
very familiar to you.

00:51:04.270 --> 00:51:07.740
This is exactly the same
f dot P inner product

00:51:07.740 --> 00:51:09.468
structure as you saw before.

00:51:09.468 --> 00:51:11.260
P of i is defined a
little bit differently.

00:51:11.260 --> 00:51:12.645
That's s of i of t.

00:51:12.645 --> 00:51:15.070
But that should be familiar
from earlier as well.

00:51:15.070 --> 00:51:17.470
That's the softmax pre top-k.

00:51:17.470 --> 00:51:21.060
So hopefully, this looks
all pretty good to you.

00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:22.740
The other thing you
might want, though,

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:25.150
is you might want to
balance across experts.

00:51:25.150 --> 00:51:27.210
That's all well and
good, but you might also

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:29.510
want to think about
the system's concerns,

00:51:29.510 --> 00:51:31.260
because you're going
to shard your experts

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:33.330
onto different
devices and you might

00:51:33.330 --> 00:51:35.390
want to balance per device.

00:51:35.390 --> 00:51:37.620
And so you might have
another loss that's

00:51:37.620 --> 00:51:39.550
essentially the same structure.

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:43.230
But instead of summing which
tokens go to which experts,

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:46.470
you might measure, which
tokens go to which devices?

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:48.300
And that's going to be
a different f that's

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:52.330
measured over the device groups
rather than over each expert.

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:54.660
And so now you can set
up a different loss

00:51:54.660 --> 00:51:56.590
to balance over devices.

00:51:56.590 --> 00:51:57.700
You optimize this.

00:51:57.700 --> 00:52:00.360
You're naturally going to try
to learn routing functions that

00:52:00.360 --> 00:52:04.350
make sure each GPU or
each TPU, what have you,

00:52:04.350 --> 00:52:07.560
have an even number of tokens,
leading to even utilization.

00:52:07.560 --> 00:52:10.710
And that would be great
from a systems perspective.

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:16.200
So basically everyone
does this kind of a thing.

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:20.770
And so DeepSeek-V3 actually
kind of innovates a little bit.

00:52:20.770 --> 00:52:21.953
This is kind of cool.

00:52:21.953 --> 00:52:23.620
And I don't think
I've seen this before.

00:52:23.620 --> 00:52:26.520
It's one of the first
things in the MoE world

00:52:26.520 --> 00:52:28.650
that doesn't actually
come from Google really,

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:33.860
which is that they have gotten
rid of this per-expert balancing

00:52:33.860 --> 00:52:34.360
term.

00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:36.480
They've gotten rid
of this entirely.

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:39.510
And instead, what they
now do is they basically

00:52:39.510 --> 00:52:43.470
take their softmax scores and
they add a little fudge factor,

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:46.550
b of i, where b of i is
a little fudge factor

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:48.170
score for each expert.

00:52:48.170 --> 00:52:51.450
So expert i might get
upweighted or downweighted.

00:52:51.450 --> 00:52:54.650
So if an expert isn't
getting enough tokens,

00:52:54.650 --> 00:52:57.830
it's going to be
given a higher b of i,

00:52:57.830 --> 00:53:01.040
and then that's going to
allow it to grab more tokens.

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:06.470
And the way that this
works is that they're

00:53:06.470 --> 00:53:09.950
going to learn b of i through
a really simple online gradient

00:53:09.950 --> 00:53:11.370
scheme, online learning.

00:53:11.370 --> 00:53:14.603
And so they're going to
measure at each batch, what are

00:53:14.603 --> 00:53:15.770
each of the experts getting?

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:18.142
Are they getting an
even number of tokens?

00:53:18.142 --> 00:53:19.850
And if they're not
getting enough tokens,

00:53:19.850 --> 00:53:23.240
they add gamma, some
learning rate to b of i,

00:53:23.240 --> 00:53:24.500
making it higher.

00:53:24.500 --> 00:53:26.030
If they're getting
too many tokens,

00:53:26.030 --> 00:53:29.180
they're going to subtract gamma,
making that expert slightly less

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:29.790
attractive.

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:32.420
So they're just learning little
offsets for each of the s

00:53:32.420 --> 00:53:33.150
of i's.

00:53:33.150 --> 00:53:35.840
And notice here, you're
only using the b of i's

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:37.170
to make the routing decisions.

00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:39.140
You're not actually
sending it over as part

00:53:39.140 --> 00:53:40.862
of your gating weights.

00:53:40.862 --> 00:53:43.170
That's a somewhat
important thing to do.

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:45.630
So they call this auxiliary
loss-free balancing.

00:53:45.630 --> 00:53:47.790
If you go and read the
DeepSeek-V3 paper--

00:53:47.790 --> 00:53:50.960
which all of you should because
it's a really nice paper--

00:53:50.960 --> 00:53:52.340
they'll make a
big deal about how

00:53:52.340 --> 00:53:56.808
this makes training so stable,
so great, so wonderful.

00:53:56.808 --> 00:53:58.850
And then, of course, you
keep reading the section

00:53:58.850 --> 00:54:00.380
and they're like,
actually, but we

00:54:00.380 --> 00:54:02.960
decided that for each
sequence, maybe we still

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:03.840
want to be balanced.

00:54:03.840 --> 00:54:05.257
And this doesn't
work well enough.

00:54:05.257 --> 00:54:07.410
So we've added the
heuristic loss back.

00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:10.550
So they do have something called
the complementary sequence-wise

00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:11.730
auxiliary loss.

00:54:11.730 --> 00:54:14.990
That is basically exactly
the auxiliary loss

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:17.840
that they decided they needed
because what they wanted to do

00:54:17.840 --> 00:54:23.780
was to load balance the experts
at a per-sequence level rather

00:54:23.780 --> 00:54:25.353
than a per-batch level.

00:54:25.353 --> 00:54:27.770
I'm not sure why they do this
particular thing rather than

00:54:27.770 --> 00:54:32.210
any other b of i-style
trick, but that's

00:54:32.210 --> 00:54:35.070
just kind of what they
do in DeepSeek-V3.

00:54:35.070 --> 00:54:36.830
So it's not fully
auxiliary loss-free

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:39.020
as they'd like you to believe.

00:54:39.020 --> 00:54:40.241
OK.

00:54:40.241 --> 00:54:40.860
Oh, yes.

00:54:40.860 --> 00:54:41.612
Question.

00:54:41.612 --> 00:54:43.650
This is a bit of
an unfair question.

00:54:43.650 --> 00:54:47.080
But if we did not have to worry
about systems optimizations,

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:48.830
do you think the
performance of this model

00:54:48.830 --> 00:54:52.280
would be a lot better or would
it stay roughly the same?

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:54.440
If we did not think about
systems optimization,

00:54:54.440 --> 00:54:56.100
would the performance
of this model

00:54:56.100 --> 00:54:57.440
be better or stay the same?

00:54:57.440 --> 00:54:59.190
When you say this
model, what do you mean?

00:54:59.190 --> 00:54:59.870
DeepSeek-V3?

00:54:59.870 --> 00:55:02.660
Or just in general,
these modern MoEs.

00:55:02.660 --> 00:55:07.340
So are you saying, if we
ignore the system's concerns,

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:09.540
do we think MoEs are still good?

00:55:09.540 --> 00:55:12.500
Is that one way of
asking that question?

00:55:12.500 --> 00:55:14.910
Would the performance of
downstream tasks, for example,

00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:17.090
be better than what
we have right now?

00:55:17.090 --> 00:55:17.660
Yeah.

00:55:17.660 --> 00:55:19.550
So I think--

00:55:19.550 --> 00:55:21.090
If I didn't have
to balance this,

00:55:21.090 --> 00:55:23.300
I must send a
roughly equal number

00:55:23.300 --> 00:55:25.680
of tokens for every expert or--

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:26.360
Yeah.

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:26.700
That's right.

00:55:26.700 --> 00:55:27.242
That's right.

00:55:27.242 --> 00:55:30.970
Well, I think, actually,
per-expert balancing, this term,

00:55:30.970 --> 00:55:32.850
this is not a systems concern.

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:35.330
So you still want to do this,
because if you don't do this,

00:55:35.330 --> 00:55:36.660
what you'll find--

00:55:36.660 --> 00:55:37.880
and actually, there's--

00:55:37.880 --> 00:55:39.350
I'm going to keep referring
to the OlMoE paper

00:55:39.350 --> 00:55:40.950
because they have
so many ablations.

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:42.590
They have a really
nice ablation where

00:55:42.590 --> 00:55:44.600
they get rid of exactly this.

00:55:44.600 --> 00:55:48.600
And what they find is,
basically, early on in training,

00:55:48.600 --> 00:55:51.135
the model just picks one
or two experts and all

00:55:51.135 --> 00:55:52.260
the other experts are dead.

00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:53.940
The router never sends
anything to them.

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:56.360
So you're just wasting
memory at that point.

00:55:56.360 --> 00:55:58.920
So now, you've just lost
performance for free.

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:01.050
You've effectively
gotten a smaller model.

00:56:01.050 --> 00:56:03.710
And so even if you ignore all
the other device balancing,

00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:06.590
parallelism concerns, you've
just gotten a worse model

00:56:06.590 --> 00:56:09.700
because you didn't properly
allocate your experts.

00:56:09.700 --> 00:56:12.227
It's the same way as want
to use all your parameters.

00:56:12.227 --> 00:56:14.310
You would like to effectively
use your parameters.

00:56:14.310 --> 00:56:15.760
You want to do expert balancing.

00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:17.900
What does device refer to?

00:56:17.900 --> 00:56:18.440
Sorry, say--

00:56:18.440 --> 00:56:19.400
Device.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:21.390
What does the device refer to?

00:56:21.390 --> 00:56:21.890
Yeah.

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:25.860
Actually, so normally, this
would refer to GPU or TPU.

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:26.750
There is a subtlety--

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:29.083
I'll talk about this maybe
in the very last or second to

00:56:29.083 --> 00:56:29.780
last slide.

00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:31.850
There are more sophisticated
and cool versions

00:56:31.850 --> 00:56:34.700
of this where you try to balance
things to minimize communication

00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:35.670
costs as well.

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:39.740
And so there's broader notions
of device, like one rack

00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:40.560
or whatever else.

00:56:40.560 --> 00:56:43.490
But here, it usually
refers to GPU.

00:56:43.490 --> 00:56:44.630
Yes.

00:56:44.630 --> 00:56:49.280
Going back to the fact that
hashing, as a routing algorithm,

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:52.590
seems to improve performance,
is there intuition for that?

00:56:52.590 --> 00:56:59.313
Because that's effectively
just randomly choosing

00:56:59.313 --> 00:57:01.730
one of the feedforward networks
to send it through, right?

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:03.960
It's like, why does having
multiple copies of that,

00:57:03.960 --> 00:57:06.320
I guess, each of
which get less data,

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:08.160
why does that make
performance better?

00:57:08.160 --> 00:57:08.660
Yeah.

00:57:08.660 --> 00:57:12.350
So the question was, why does
hashing do anything at all?

00:57:12.350 --> 00:57:15.638
I don't have the really
precise intuition for this,

00:57:15.638 --> 00:57:17.430
but you can make
arguments either two ways.

00:57:17.430 --> 00:57:20.480
One is, even if you're
hashing, the same tokens

00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:22.320
are going to go to the same--

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:24.130
or the same kinds of
sequences, they're

00:57:24.130 --> 00:57:27.380
going to go to the
same expert every time.

00:57:27.380 --> 00:57:30.860
And so each expert will still
get some deterministic subset

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:31.603
of the inputs.

00:57:31.603 --> 00:57:33.020
And so there's
some specialization

00:57:33.020 --> 00:57:33.895
that can still occur.

00:57:33.895 --> 00:57:36.710
It's just non-semantic
or non-learned.

00:57:36.710 --> 00:57:38.720
And if you're a
distribution Zipfian,

00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:41.750
the word "the" might
dominate one expert.

00:57:41.750 --> 00:57:44.270
And so you might still get
actually semantic specialization

00:57:44.270 --> 00:57:46.220
where one expert is
effectively dominated

00:57:46.220 --> 00:57:47.720
by very frequent things.

00:57:47.720 --> 00:57:50.690
But random routing
function probably

00:57:50.690 --> 00:57:51.890
wouldn't be [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:51.890 --> 00:57:54.360
A pure random thing that's
not dependent on input?

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:54.860
Yeah.

00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:56.580
I would bet that would
be really terrible.

00:57:56.580 --> 00:57:57.080
Yes.

00:57:57.080 --> 00:57:59.290
I have never run or
seen that, but, yes, I

00:57:59.290 --> 00:58:01.200
think that would be horrible.

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:01.700
Good.

00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:02.700
Yes.

00:58:02.700 --> 00:58:06.200
So for more [INAUDIBLE],
you have many labels or many

00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:08.900
[? transforms. ?] And I think
[INAUDIBLE] the lecture,

00:58:08.900 --> 00:58:12.080
you mentioned that each expert
[INAUDIBLE] could be used.

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:16.770
So if you have a document
like 32 layers or 64 experts,

00:58:16.770 --> 00:58:18.450
that's like a model you can use.

00:58:18.450 --> 00:58:22.120
Or I wonder if a couple experts
are bundled together on a single

00:58:22.120 --> 00:58:24.120
GPU, is that going
[? to need ?] [? coverage? ?]

00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:24.200
Yeah.

00:58:24.200 --> 00:58:26.420
So the question was like,
won't you need lots of GPUs

00:58:26.420 --> 00:58:28.378
if you have lots of layers
and lots of experts?

00:58:28.378 --> 00:58:32.220
Yeah, if you exclusively give
a GPU to a single expert,

00:58:32.220 --> 00:58:34.520
yes, that would
be kind of crazy.

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:36.020
But you would kind
of shard things

00:58:36.020 --> 00:58:39.410
so that each GPU would
hold enough of these units

00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:41.462
to effectively use memory.

00:58:41.462 --> 00:58:42.920
The name of the
game in parallelism

00:58:42.920 --> 00:58:44.930
is you always want to
use up all of your memory

00:58:44.930 --> 00:58:46.472
because that's one
of your resources.

00:58:46.472 --> 00:58:49.730
You don't want to parallelize
more than you have to.

00:58:49.730 --> 00:58:54.415
Cool, OK, Excellent Oh, OK, I
did put the ablation in here.

00:58:54.415 --> 00:58:56.540
So this is exactly what
happens to the question of,

00:58:56.540 --> 00:59:01.080
what happens if you don't
do expert balancing loss?

00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:03.660
I think the great picture to
see is this bottom left one.

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:06.830
If you don't do load
balancing, what are the tokens

00:59:06.830 --> 00:59:07.980
assigned to which expert?

00:59:07.980 --> 00:59:09.930
You see the pink and
the yellow expert.

00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:11.580
They just kind take over.

00:59:11.580 --> 00:59:14.070
They take up about
50% of the tokens.

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:15.510
All the other experts are dead.

00:59:15.510 --> 00:59:16.290
They do nothing.

00:59:16.290 --> 00:59:19.490
And so you've wasted the
majority of your experts

00:59:19.490 --> 00:59:22.520
at this point, six out
of eight of your experts.

00:59:22.520 --> 00:59:26.310
And you've created a
two-expert MoE unintentionally.

00:59:26.310 --> 00:59:31.230
And that gives you worse
losses, seen on the top right,

00:59:31.230 --> 00:59:32.575
the teal lines.

00:59:32.575 --> 00:59:34.950
Of course, maybe that's still
better than the dense model

00:59:34.950 --> 00:59:37.470
because at least you've
got two experts going.

00:59:37.470 --> 00:59:40.190
But you could have done better,
counterfactually speaking.

00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:47.310
So I won't go quite as deep
as I could into the system

00:59:47.310 --> 00:59:49.350
side because I
haven't really started

00:59:49.350 --> 00:59:52.680
to cover the core systems
concepts necessary

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:55.230
for you to deeply appreciate
a lot of the parallelism

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:58.260
concerns, basically the
hierarchy of communication

00:59:58.260 --> 01:00:00.960
speeds in a data
center and so on.

01:00:00.960 --> 01:00:04.260
But really, as I said before,
one thing to keep in mind

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:08.532
is just how nicely MoE's
can fit into devices.

01:00:08.532 --> 01:00:11.310
The thing that people
say is expert parallel,

01:00:11.310 --> 01:00:18.490
that involves putting one or a
few experts onto each device.

01:00:18.490 --> 01:00:22.180
And what happens when you are
basically processing a token?

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:23.820
Well, you would hit the router.

01:00:23.820 --> 01:00:27.880
And after the router, you
now have picked few experts.

01:00:27.880 --> 01:00:30.430
And so now, you would have a
collective communication call,

01:00:30.430 --> 01:00:32.880
like all to all
communication dispatch, that

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:35.990
would send the tokens
to the relevant devices,

01:00:35.990 --> 01:00:38.970
the feed forwards to
compute their outputs.

01:00:38.970 --> 01:00:43.070
And then you would return the
tokens to where they belong.

01:00:43.070 --> 01:00:46.080
Or you would combine, I
guess, multiple experts.

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:48.960
And so you would need another
collective communication call.

01:00:48.960 --> 01:00:51.200
And so if your feed
forward computations

01:00:51.200 --> 01:00:53.060
are of big and beefy
enough, you can

01:00:53.060 --> 01:00:57.890
pay for the cost of basically
doing this expert parallelism.

01:00:57.890 --> 01:01:00.680
And the thing that's
nice about this

01:01:00.680 --> 01:01:04.500
is that it's another form of
parallelism in your toolkit.

01:01:04.500 --> 01:01:08.400
So you've got, on the right
side, data parallelism,

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:11.340
model parallelism of two
or three different kinds.

01:01:11.340 --> 01:01:13.000
And then you've got
expert parallelism.

01:01:13.000 --> 01:01:14.750
And you can combine
all of them to come up

01:01:14.750 --> 01:01:18.060
with ways of trading off
all the resources you have.

01:01:18.060 --> 01:01:20.760
So the communication speed, the
amount of data that you have,

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:24.990
your batch size, and your number
of experts and your memory.

01:01:24.990 --> 01:01:28.392
So I'm not going to go into
too much detail about how

01:01:28.392 --> 01:01:29.850
specifically this
is going to help.

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:33.770
But keep in mind that this gives
you another tool in your expert

01:01:33.770 --> 01:01:35.570
toolkit.

01:01:35.570 --> 01:01:39.920
Another thing that
is also useful

01:01:39.920 --> 01:01:44.400
is let's say you have multiple
experts on a single device.

01:01:44.400 --> 01:01:47.940
You might hope that because the
computations are sparse-- like,

01:01:47.940 --> 01:01:53.190
let's say, this first token,
gets multiplied to expert 0.

01:01:53.190 --> 01:01:54.360
The second one is expert 1.

01:01:54.360 --> 01:01:55.680
And this third one is expert 2.

01:01:55.680 --> 01:01:58.550
So this is really
three matrix multiplies

01:01:58.550 --> 01:02:00.210
that are small and sparse.

01:02:00.210 --> 01:02:02.180
And you might hope
that modern GPUs

01:02:02.180 --> 01:02:08.990
can take advantage of these
kinds of sparse matrix

01:02:08.990 --> 01:02:10.370
multiplications.

01:02:10.370 --> 01:02:11.760
And that's exactly right.

01:02:11.760 --> 01:02:15.170
So if you lay out
your experts correctly

01:02:15.170 --> 01:02:17.340
and the weights are of
fused in the right way,

01:02:17.340 --> 01:02:21.200
then modern sparse
matrix multiply engines

01:02:21.200 --> 01:02:23.690
can effectively make
sure that you're not

01:02:23.690 --> 01:02:27.270
wasting any FLOPs in doing
this one big matrix multiply.

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:29.390
So modern libraries
like MegaBlocks

01:02:29.390 --> 01:02:32.910
can basically take advantage
of this device level sparsity

01:02:32.910 --> 01:02:37.150
support to do multiple expert
computations all at once.

01:02:37.150 --> 01:02:43.410
So this is yet another advantage
that you get with MoE's.

01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:48.630
So one fun side thing, which
maybe isn't mysterious to you

01:02:48.630 --> 01:02:52.680
anymore because you've grown
up in the era of GPT-4--

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:55.990
but when the GPT-4
API first came out,

01:02:55.990 --> 01:02:57.780
it was kind of mysterious to me.

01:02:57.780 --> 01:03:00.360
Because when you set
the temperature to 0,

01:03:00.360 --> 01:03:02.550
you got different responses
even though it was

01:03:02.550 --> 01:03:04.830
supposed to be deterministic.

01:03:04.830 --> 01:03:08.460
And lots of people speculated
about why would that be.

01:03:08.460 --> 01:03:11.620
I'm not saying this is
the answer to that reason.

01:03:11.620 --> 01:03:14.580
But there is actually an
interesting source of randomness

01:03:14.580 --> 01:03:15.790
in MoE's.

01:03:15.790 --> 01:03:18.040
So in MoE's, think
about what happens.

01:03:18.040 --> 01:03:19.980
You're going to route
your tokens to experts.

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:22.560
And experts live in
different devices.

01:03:22.560 --> 01:03:25.233
It could be that you
have a lot of examples.

01:03:25.233 --> 01:03:27.150
You're going to, of
course, batch your queries

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:28.420
when you're processing them.

01:03:28.420 --> 01:03:30.290
And so if you've
batched your queries,

01:03:30.290 --> 01:03:32.790
these tokens are going to get
routed into different experts.

01:03:32.790 --> 01:03:36.500
So imagine you've got
this batch to process.

01:03:36.500 --> 01:03:38.040
And you've got a
bunch of experts.

01:03:38.040 --> 01:03:40.550
But for whatever
reason, this batch

01:03:40.550 --> 01:03:42.480
really loves expert number 3.

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:44.700
All the tokens go
to expert number 3.

01:03:44.700 --> 01:03:46.050
So now, what happens?

01:03:46.050 --> 01:03:47.990
Well, the device
for expert number 3

01:03:47.990 --> 01:03:51.890
doesn't have enough memory
to load all of those tokens.

01:03:51.890 --> 01:03:55.380
And then what happens is what
people call token dropping.

01:03:55.380 --> 01:03:57.390
And this happens at
training time as well.

01:03:57.390 --> 01:03:59.900
You often have what's
called a load factor, where

01:03:59.900 --> 01:04:02.910
you're controlling the maximum
number of allowed tokens.

01:04:02.910 --> 01:04:06.300
And if the router just allocates
too many tokens to an expert,

01:04:06.300 --> 01:04:09.170
you just drop those tokens
off, either for systems reasons

01:04:09.170 --> 01:04:11.780
or because you're just
worried that that expert is

01:04:11.780 --> 01:04:14.060
going to take over, at
least in the training time.

01:04:14.060 --> 01:04:16.550
So now, this token
has gotten dropped.

01:04:16.550 --> 01:04:18.300
And it's not going to
get anything at all.

01:04:18.300 --> 01:04:20.192
The MLP is just going
to do a 0 computation.

01:04:20.192 --> 01:04:21.650
And the residual
connection is just

01:04:21.650 --> 01:04:23.720
going to pass things
straight forward.

01:04:23.720 --> 01:04:25.530
And then you're going
to return an output.

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:26.990
And so if your
token got dropped,

01:04:26.990 --> 01:04:29.120
you're going to get a different
result than if your token didn't

01:04:29.120 --> 01:04:29.820
get dropped.

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:32.780
And so based on who
else is in your batch,

01:04:32.780 --> 01:04:35.775
MoE's can induce stochasticity
both at training time

01:04:35.775 --> 01:04:38.150
and inference time, which is
kind of an interesting thing

01:04:38.150 --> 01:04:39.608
that you don't
normally think about

01:04:39.608 --> 01:04:42.560
because you almost never think
about cross batch effects

01:04:42.560 --> 01:04:47.165
when doing inference.

01:04:47.165 --> 01:04:51.950
So that's the main bits of
the main basic components

01:04:51.950 --> 01:04:54.750
of building the MoE
in a fun side thing.

01:04:54.750 --> 01:04:57.020
If you were to actually
go out tomorrow and try

01:04:57.020 --> 01:04:59.638
to train an MoE, I
think the system side

01:04:59.638 --> 01:05:00.930
will make you a little bit sad.

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:02.722
But the other thing
that would make you sad

01:05:02.722 --> 01:05:05.720
is probably the
stability side of things.

01:05:05.720 --> 01:05:09.470
So MoE's is kind of have this
property that sometimes they'll

01:05:09.470 --> 01:05:12.140
just kind of blow up on you
if you try to fine tune them.

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:13.640
They're very difficult
to fine tune.

01:05:13.640 --> 01:05:16.070
And they'll sometimes
blow up on you.

01:05:16.070 --> 01:05:21.180
And so Barret Zoph and
others really studied.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:23.420
They had a whole paper
on basically trying

01:05:23.420 --> 01:05:24.990
to make MoE's more stable.

01:05:24.990 --> 01:05:28.520
And there's a paper, which is
the one I'm referencing here,

01:05:28.520 --> 01:05:31.980
whose entire purpose is
to stabilize MoE training.

01:05:31.980 --> 01:05:33.830
And there's a couple
of tricks that I'll

01:05:33.830 --> 01:05:37.910
mention that I think are
relevant in that people do.

01:05:37.910 --> 01:05:41.730
The first one is if you're
doing the router softmax--

01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:44.180
so this goes back to last
lecture about stability.

01:05:44.180 --> 01:05:45.630
What did I say about stability?

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:48.617
Well, the thing to be
afraid of is the softmaxes.

01:05:48.617 --> 01:05:50.700
The softmax is always where
you want to be afraid.

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:55.340
And so for the MoE's, they
do all the computations

01:05:55.340 --> 01:06:00.472
in float32 for the router
computations just to be safe.

01:06:00.472 --> 01:06:04.465
And sometimes, they also
add an auxiliary z-loss.

01:06:04.465 --> 01:06:06.840
So hopefully, you remember
that it was just last lecture.

01:06:06.840 --> 01:06:10.790
You do log of the sum of
the exponentiated values

01:06:10.790 --> 01:06:11.520
in the softmax.

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:11.990
And you square that.

01:06:11.990 --> 01:06:13.470
And you add that
as an extra loss.

01:06:13.470 --> 01:06:16.650
So this is going to keep the
normalizer values near 1,

01:06:16.650 --> 01:06:18.068
which is nice for stability.

01:06:18.068 --> 01:06:19.610
So this is actually
one of the places

01:06:19.610 --> 01:06:22.580
where z-loss was used
earlier before it got more

01:06:22.580 --> 01:06:25.500
popular for training models.

01:06:25.500 --> 01:06:27.260
You can see the effects here.

01:06:27.260 --> 01:06:31.010
If you look at the losses,
I think the second plot here

01:06:31.010 --> 01:06:32.460
is maybe a great one.

01:06:32.460 --> 01:06:36.660
If you remove the z-loss
from your routing function,

01:06:36.660 --> 01:06:41.300
you see this giant loss spikes
in your validation loss, where

01:06:41.300 --> 01:06:44.120
the model just kind of goes a
little bit crazy for a couple

01:06:44.120 --> 01:06:46.290
iterations and then gets
kind of pulled back.

01:06:46.290 --> 01:06:48.210
Of course, it still trains OK.

01:06:48.210 --> 01:06:49.958
But you are better
off having the z-loss

01:06:49.958 --> 01:06:51.000
than not having a z-loss.

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.660
There is a pretty noticeable
gap in the validation

01:06:53.660 --> 01:06:54.785
loss by the end here.

01:06:57.980 --> 01:07:01.400
Other things that can happen.

01:07:01.400 --> 01:07:03.180
Of course, you want
to fine tune your MoE.

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:05.210
You'd like to also
RLHF your MoE if you're

01:07:05.210 --> 01:07:07.190
going to ship and release it.

01:07:07.190 --> 01:07:11.130
But this turns out
to be problematic.

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:14.060
Some of the earlier work, when
people were starting to do

01:07:14.060 --> 01:07:16.950
MoE's-- this was back
in the BERT and T5 era.

01:07:16.950 --> 01:07:19.700
So there was a lot of
fine tuning going on.

01:07:19.700 --> 01:07:23.540
And one of the things that
people saw was actually

01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:26.030
there's a lot of overfitting
that happens if you were

01:07:26.030 --> 01:07:27.930
kind of doing sparse models.

01:07:27.930 --> 01:07:30.080
You see this big gap
between train and val,

01:07:30.080 --> 01:07:32.180
this blue and orange line.

01:07:32.180 --> 01:07:34.670
Whereas the dense model,
this green and red line,

01:07:34.670 --> 01:07:36.925
has a smaller train test gap.

01:07:36.925 --> 01:07:38.300
And so there was
a lot of worries

01:07:38.300 --> 01:07:41.150
about overfitting because you
have these gigantic parameter

01:07:41.150 --> 01:07:43.640
models that you're fine
tuning on small data.

01:07:43.640 --> 01:07:46.380
One of the solutions that
was proposed at the time--

01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:49.340
I don't think this is very
popular as far as I understand--

01:07:49.340 --> 01:07:53.860
is to architect your MoE's such
that not every layer is an MoE

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:54.360
layer.

01:07:54.360 --> 01:07:57.802
But you, let's say, alternate
dense layers and MoE layers.

01:07:57.802 --> 01:07:59.510
Then you just fine
tune the dense layers.

01:07:59.510 --> 01:08:01.280
And then that will
still be fine.

01:08:01.280 --> 01:08:03.530
That behaves just
like a dense model.

01:08:03.530 --> 01:08:04.680
So that was fine.

01:08:04.680 --> 01:08:06.770
Another solution,
the one that we

01:08:06.770 --> 01:08:10.230
saw in the DeepSeek MoE paper,
is just use a lot of data.

01:08:10.230 --> 01:08:12.560
If overfitting is a
problem, we have access

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:14.340
to lots and lots of SFT data.

01:08:14.340 --> 01:08:15.960
Just shovel all
of those guys in.

01:08:15.960 --> 01:08:19.550
So in the case of DeepSeek MoE,
they use 1.4 million training

01:08:19.550 --> 01:08:21.020
examples.

01:08:21.020 --> 01:08:24.319
Then maybe you're not quite as
worried about these overfitting

01:08:24.319 --> 01:08:26.630
concerns.

01:08:26.630 --> 01:08:30.770
The last thing I'll end with,
which is a trick in the toolkit

01:08:30.770 --> 01:08:34.890
that people have done
and seen, is upcycling.

01:08:34.890 --> 01:08:38.390
And so this idea is
to take a dense model,

01:08:38.390 --> 01:08:40.160
like the one over here.

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:41.689
And then you take your MLP.

01:08:41.689 --> 01:08:44.330
And you make a bunch
of copies of it.

01:08:44.330 --> 01:08:46.080
And then you maybe perturb it.

01:08:46.080 --> 01:08:49.439
And then you have your router
that's initialized from scratch.

01:08:49.439 --> 01:08:51.359
And then you just
pretend this is an MoE.

01:08:51.359 --> 01:08:53.149
And then you train it
from that point on.

01:08:53.149 --> 01:08:55.979
Just initialize the
MoE from a dense model.

01:08:55.979 --> 01:08:59.180
And this is a trick that's
kind of called upcycling.

01:08:59.180 --> 01:09:02.970
And people have shown that
if you can get it to work,

01:09:02.970 --> 01:09:05.510
it is a very, very,
very cost-effective way

01:09:05.510 --> 01:09:06.859
of getting a MoE.

01:09:06.859 --> 01:09:08.600
And the MoE is
great for inference

01:09:08.600 --> 01:09:13.439
because not every MLP is going
to be active at inference time.

01:09:13.439 --> 01:09:16.819
So you might effectively get
a much larger parameter model

01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:20.240
without doing the training of
a much larger parameter model.

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:23.000
And several people
have succeeded at this.

01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:26.870
MiniCPM, which I'll mention
again in the scaling law

01:09:26.870 --> 01:09:30.140
lecture-- but this
is a Chinese OpenLLM

01:09:30.140 --> 01:09:33.819
that basically tried to build
really good small language

01:09:33.819 --> 01:09:34.319
models.

01:09:34.319 --> 01:09:36.260
And they succeeded at
taking a dense model

01:09:36.260 --> 01:09:37.912
and upcycling it into an MoE.

01:09:37.912 --> 01:09:40.370
And you can see that their
numbers get significantly better

01:09:40.370 --> 01:09:41.450
in the last two rows.

01:09:41.450 --> 01:09:43.130
So the dense model
to the MoE, they

01:09:43.130 --> 01:09:46.340
get a pretty non-trivial
bump in performance.

01:09:46.340 --> 01:09:49.200
Qwen, I mentioned at the
start of this lecture.

01:09:49.200 --> 01:09:51.020
One of their earliest
attempts at MoE

01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:53.189
was taking one of
their dense models

01:09:53.189 --> 01:09:55.910
and then building upcycled MoE.

01:09:55.910 --> 01:09:59.270
And they got fairly
significant performance gains

01:09:59.270 --> 01:10:02.340
relative to smaller
models at the time.

01:10:02.340 --> 01:10:05.900
They got models on par with
their 7B models with a 2.7

01:10:05.900 --> 01:10:07.720
billion parameter active model.

01:10:10.760 --> 01:10:17.120
So to wrap up, I want to
walk through the DeepSeek MoE

01:10:17.120 --> 01:10:19.520
architecture at
the very end here.

01:10:19.520 --> 01:10:21.475
And hopefully, this will
give you a sense of--

01:10:21.475 --> 01:10:22.850
the first thing
I want to do is I

01:10:22.850 --> 01:10:26.090
want you to understand the
DeepSeek-V3 architecture

01:10:26.090 --> 01:10:28.640
setup and all the changes
that they did because that's

01:10:28.640 --> 01:10:32.090
an example of a modern,
high-performance open source

01:10:32.090 --> 01:10:32.850
system.

01:10:32.850 --> 01:10:35.330
I also want you to maybe
appreciate that architectures

01:10:35.330 --> 01:10:36.510
don't change that much.

01:10:36.510 --> 01:10:41.700
DeepSeek v1 or
DeepSeek MoE v1 is--

01:10:41.700 --> 01:10:43.380
it's not that new.

01:10:43.380 --> 01:10:45.720
It's like maybe a year
and a half or something,

01:10:45.720 --> 01:10:47.450
maybe two years old.

01:10:47.450 --> 01:10:50.460
And they basically nailed the
architecture at that point.

01:10:50.460 --> 01:10:52.610
So I want you to see
what they changed

01:10:52.610 --> 01:10:56.430
from that very earliest attempt
to their big training run.

01:10:56.430 --> 01:10:58.770
So this is the very
first starting point.

01:10:58.770 --> 01:11:00.540
This is DeepSeek MoE.

01:11:00.540 --> 01:11:01.290
I'm calling it v1.

01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:03.415
But actually, probably the
right way to refer to it

01:11:03.415 --> 01:11:04.220
is DeepSeek MoE.

01:11:04.220 --> 01:11:07.550
It's a 16-billion parameter
model with 2.8 of those

01:11:07.550 --> 01:11:09.260
parameters active.

01:11:09.260 --> 01:11:11.580
And you've seen already
this diagram over here.

01:11:11.580 --> 01:11:17.870
This is the two shared plus
64 fine-grained experts,

01:11:17.870 --> 01:11:21.230
of which four of them
are active at a time.

01:11:21.230 --> 01:11:23.930
Or maybe about six of them
are active at a time, sorry.

01:11:23.930 --> 01:11:26.310
And the routing, you've
already seen this.

01:11:26.310 --> 01:11:28.560
I presented this in the
middle of the lecture here.

01:11:28.560 --> 01:11:30.650
This is the very
standard top-K routing,

01:11:30.650 --> 01:11:32.180
where the softmax
is at the bottom

01:11:32.180 --> 01:11:34.400
before the top-L selection.

01:11:34.400 --> 01:11:36.770
And for balancing,
at training time,

01:11:36.770 --> 01:11:38.810
all they do is to add
this auxiliary loss

01:11:38.810 --> 01:11:42.160
balancing term, both the expert
and device level balancing

01:11:42.160 --> 01:11:42.660
terms.

01:11:42.660 --> 01:11:45.210
So hopefully, you remember
those from earlier.

01:11:45.210 --> 01:11:46.970
So that's the v1.

01:11:46.970 --> 01:11:51.810
And then they saw how
effective their MoE model was.

01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:54.120
So I guess to add
some more context,

01:11:54.120 --> 01:11:56.850
DeepSeek originally
had a dense model.

01:11:56.850 --> 01:11:58.410
And then they had an MoE model.

01:11:58.410 --> 01:12:00.750
And the MoE model
was remarkably good.

01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:04.790
And so when they went to v2,
they went straight to the MoE.

01:12:04.790 --> 01:12:08.490
And now, this is a
236-billion parameter model,

01:12:08.490 --> 01:12:11.580
of which 21 of those billion
parameters are active.

01:12:11.580 --> 01:12:13.350
So you need a lot of memory.

01:12:13.350 --> 01:12:16.160
But your FLOPs consumption
for inferencing this model

01:12:16.160 --> 01:12:17.350
is not so bad.

01:12:17.350 --> 01:12:20.120
Now, the architecture
is identical.

01:12:20.120 --> 01:12:21.790
I copied literally
the same figure

01:12:21.790 --> 01:12:23.350
because the architecture
is literally

01:12:23.350 --> 01:12:27.610
the same minus changes
to the number of experts

01:12:27.610 --> 01:12:29.470
that are active.

01:12:29.470 --> 01:12:34.120
And we've got now some new
things happening but not

01:12:34.120 --> 01:12:35.360
too many new things.

01:12:35.360 --> 01:12:38.060
So the top case
selector is the same.

01:12:38.060 --> 01:12:40.570
So the equation from before,
this previous equation, this

01:12:40.570 --> 01:12:41.410
is identical.

01:12:41.410 --> 01:12:43.690
This is still how
they do things.

01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:48.130
But they have this very
clever trick that they add on.

01:12:48.130 --> 01:12:50.500
I was going to say at the
very beginning, what's

01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:52.490
the drawback of having
fine-grained experts?

01:12:52.490 --> 01:12:55.570
Why can't I have, I don't know,
1,024 fine-grained experts

01:12:55.570 --> 01:12:57.440
or 2,046 fine-grained experts?

01:12:57.440 --> 01:13:00.940
Well, the problem is when you
shard your experts very finely

01:13:00.940 --> 01:13:03.130
and you have a lot
of active experts,

01:13:03.130 --> 01:13:05.110
you're going to have to
route to those experts.

01:13:05.110 --> 01:13:07.550
So your communication
costs potentially grow.

01:13:07.550 --> 01:13:09.130
And if you're very
fragmented, you

01:13:09.130 --> 01:13:12.140
might have to send a lot of
tokens to a lot of devices.

01:13:12.140 --> 01:13:16.460
And so the clever thing
they come up with is to say,

01:13:16.460 --> 01:13:19.060
I'm not just going
to, for each batch,

01:13:19.060 --> 01:13:21.370
route to the top-K
experts naively, which

01:13:21.370 --> 01:13:24.110
might force me to send my
tokens to lots of devices.

01:13:24.110 --> 01:13:25.870
What I'm going to
do is I'm going

01:13:25.870 --> 01:13:28.520
to first pick top-M devices.

01:13:28.520 --> 01:13:31.340
So I'm going to do my
normal scoring calculation.

01:13:31.340 --> 01:13:34.900
But I'm first going to subset
the set of allowed devices

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:37.780
to top-M. And once
I've picked my devices,

01:13:37.780 --> 01:13:41.115
then I'm going to pick top-K for
each token within each device.

01:13:41.115 --> 01:13:43.130
So now, I've
restricted the devices.

01:13:43.130 --> 01:13:45.500
This really controls
the communication cost.

01:13:45.500 --> 01:13:47.650
And now, this gives you
more efficient training

01:13:47.650 --> 01:13:50.030
when you're scaling up
to these gigantic sizes.

01:13:50.030 --> 01:13:52.450
You need to start really
engaging with the systems aspect

01:13:52.450 --> 01:13:56.230
of things when you're training
a 236-billion parameter model.

01:13:56.230 --> 01:13:59.530
The other thing which reflects
the systems concerns that

01:13:59.530 --> 01:14:02.440
are necessary at this scale is
that they add a communication

01:14:02.440 --> 01:14:04.240
balancing loss.

01:14:04.240 --> 01:14:06.950
One way of thinking about
things is, for an expert,

01:14:06.950 --> 01:14:08.620
there's inputs and outputs.

01:14:08.620 --> 01:14:10.900
The inputs are the
token comes in.

01:14:10.900 --> 01:14:12.130
And you route to your expert.

01:14:12.130 --> 01:14:15.040
And the outputs are you have
to bring the tokens back

01:14:15.040 --> 01:14:16.060
where they belong.

01:14:16.060 --> 01:14:17.870
So if a batch belongs
on this device,

01:14:17.870 --> 01:14:20.110
it has to go back where
the original device was.

01:14:20.110 --> 01:14:22.360
So we have to think about
both the input communication

01:14:22.360 --> 01:14:24.230
cost and the output
communication cost.

01:14:24.230 --> 01:14:26.410
And so they add a
balancing loss to try

01:14:26.410 --> 01:14:28.870
to balance out the output
communication cost as well, not

01:14:28.870 --> 01:14:30.350
just the input side.

01:14:30.350 --> 01:14:32.060
So that's a minor note.

01:14:32.060 --> 01:14:34.090
But you can see their
attention to detail

01:14:34.090 --> 01:14:37.630
on trying to make sure all of
the different systems aspects

01:14:37.630 --> 01:14:40.420
are properly taken care of.

01:14:40.420 --> 01:14:45.170
And then finally, we get
to the big DeepSeek-V3--

01:14:45.170 --> 01:14:47.150
sorry, that should say
V3, not V2 up there--

01:14:47.150 --> 01:14:51.730
671 billion parameters,
of which 37 are active.

01:14:51.730 --> 01:14:54.710
Once again, exactly
the same figure

01:14:54.710 --> 01:14:56.960
because the MoE architecture
itself doesn't change.

01:14:56.960 --> 01:14:58.780
That's stayed the same
since DeepSeek MoE.

01:14:58.780 --> 01:15:00.580
If it works, don't change it.

01:15:00.580 --> 01:15:02.650
They do change a
couple of things.

01:15:02.650 --> 01:15:04.970
Maybe they were
hearing you all say,

01:15:04.970 --> 01:15:06.620
why don't you normalize to one?

01:15:06.620 --> 01:15:08.600
And so they've normalized
the gate to one.

01:15:08.600 --> 01:15:10.900
They've moved kind of the
softmax normalizer operation

01:15:10.900 --> 01:15:11.860
up there.

01:15:11.860 --> 01:15:14.170
But they're not
actually exponentiating

01:15:14.170 --> 01:15:16.360
the gating decisions.

01:15:16.360 --> 01:15:18.100
They're actually
taking sigmoids,

01:15:18.100 --> 01:15:23.080
which is a softer, more
nicely behaved operation

01:15:23.080 --> 01:15:24.080
than the softmax.

01:15:24.080 --> 01:15:25.823
And so they've got
some changes here.

01:15:25.823 --> 01:15:27.490
But conceptually,
this is still the same

01:15:27.490 --> 01:15:28.880
as the top-K routing decision.

01:15:28.880 --> 01:15:32.200
You hopefully see very, very
similar things happening.

01:15:32.200 --> 01:15:35.260
And then in terms of
the losses, they've

01:15:35.260 --> 01:15:38.380
gone to this auxiliary
loss-free trick of this b

01:15:38.380 --> 01:15:40.270
of i being incremented
or decremented

01:15:40.270 --> 01:15:41.810
based on the expert load.

01:15:41.810 --> 01:15:44.980
And then they have a
sequence-wise auxiliary loss.

01:15:44.980 --> 01:15:47.080
And just to add some
context, why would you

01:15:47.080 --> 01:15:51.860
want to balance different
experts on a single sequence?

01:15:51.860 --> 01:15:54.040
Well, the thing that
they're very concerned about

01:15:54.040 --> 01:15:57.340
is that training time,
it's fine to not have

01:15:57.340 --> 01:15:58.910
a sequence wise balancing loss.

01:15:58.910 --> 01:16:00.940
But at inference time,
it might be the case

01:16:00.940 --> 01:16:03.820
that someone sends you very
out-of-distribution sequences.

01:16:03.820 --> 01:16:06.010
And that might overwhelm
certain experts.

01:16:06.010 --> 01:16:08.960
So at inference time, you can't
control which sequences you get.

01:16:08.960 --> 01:16:11.560
So you might want
stronger balancing

01:16:11.560 --> 01:16:13.780
that operates at a single
sequence level rather

01:16:13.780 --> 01:16:15.480
than an overall batch level.

01:16:18.960 --> 01:16:19.460
Oh, sorry.

01:16:19.460 --> 01:16:20.980
Yes.

01:16:20.980 --> 01:16:25.730
Does V3 still do
the top-M devices?

01:16:25.730 --> 01:16:28.180
But does it keep the v2?

01:16:28.180 --> 01:16:30.470
Yeah, they keep the
top-M improvement.

01:16:30.470 --> 01:16:33.320
They do not keep, for example,
the communication loss.

01:16:33.320 --> 01:16:35.270
So they've jettisoned
some things.

01:16:35.270 --> 01:16:38.000
But top-M is-- I mean, it
seems like a pretty clever idea

01:16:38.000 --> 01:16:38.630
to keep it.

01:16:42.260 --> 01:16:44.120
But it's not like they
always add things.

01:16:44.120 --> 01:16:47.410
They have removed
some of the things.

01:16:47.410 --> 01:16:50.750
And so in the last two or
so minutes of the class,

01:16:50.750 --> 01:16:54.150
I'm going to go over the
non-MoE parts of DeepSeek-V3

01:16:54.150 --> 01:16:55.900
because I think we're
already at the point

01:16:55.900 --> 01:16:57.950
where I've explained
most of DeepSeek-V3.

01:16:57.950 --> 01:16:59.470
I might as well go
through the steps

01:16:59.470 --> 01:17:01.790
of explaining the rest of
DeepSeek-V3 at this point

01:17:01.790 --> 01:17:03.680
so you all know how that works.

01:17:03.680 --> 01:17:07.690
So they have a
clever optimization

01:17:07.690 --> 01:17:09.370
for the attention
piece called MLA,

01:17:09.370 --> 01:17:11.300
or Multi-head Latent Attention.

01:17:11.300 --> 01:17:14.445
And you all actually already
know all the ingredients

01:17:14.445 --> 01:17:15.820
that you need to
understand this.

01:17:15.820 --> 01:17:17.710
Because at the end
of last lecture,

01:17:17.710 --> 01:17:19.840
I talked about GQA and MHA.

01:17:19.840 --> 01:17:22.000
So those are all
inference optimizations

01:17:22.000 --> 01:17:23.830
that you need in
order to optimize

01:17:23.830 --> 01:17:25.400
the size of the KV cache.

01:17:25.400 --> 01:17:28.000
So the DeepSeek folks
take a different tack

01:17:28.000 --> 01:17:30.050
or different approach
at optimizing this.

01:17:30.050 --> 01:17:32.530
Instead of reducing
the number of heads,

01:17:32.530 --> 01:17:35.260
they're actually going to
project the heads into a lower

01:17:35.260 --> 01:17:36.260
dimensional space.

01:17:36.260 --> 01:17:37.750
So you have your inputs h of t.

01:17:37.750 --> 01:17:41.050
And instead of generating
the k's and v's directly

01:17:41.050 --> 01:17:43.420
from these h of t's,
what I'm going to do

01:17:43.420 --> 01:17:46.360
is I'm going to first
generate a low dimensional c.

01:17:46.360 --> 01:17:49.760
You can think of this as
a compressed version of h.

01:17:49.760 --> 01:17:53.600
And this c is going to be
smaller and easier to cache.

01:17:53.600 --> 01:17:55.190
And I'm just going
to cache these c's.

01:17:55.190 --> 01:17:58.190
And whenever I need
these k's and v's, well,

01:17:58.190 --> 01:18:02.120
I can up project from this
KV conceptually speaking.

01:18:02.120 --> 01:18:05.120
And then I can take the
inner products with the q's.

01:18:05.120 --> 01:18:08.560
So you can see how this would
be a KV cache savings if I only

01:18:08.560 --> 01:18:12.820
have to save the c instead of
the higher dimensional h of t.

01:18:12.820 --> 01:18:15.590
And that's exactly the idea.

01:18:15.590 --> 01:18:16.760
So you take your h of t.

01:18:16.760 --> 01:18:19.400
You project it into a
lower dimensional c.

01:18:19.400 --> 01:18:23.180
And then you up project this
back into the k's and v's.

01:18:23.180 --> 01:18:26.720
And if the c's are small, well,
you've compressed the KV cache.

01:18:26.720 --> 01:18:28.000
That's good.

01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:30.880
And then in terms
of the computation,

01:18:30.880 --> 01:18:33.572
if you're thinking about
FLOPs, well, you might think,

01:18:33.572 --> 01:18:36.280
well, this is not good because I
have to multiply an extra matrix

01:18:36.280 --> 01:18:37.640
WUK.

01:18:37.640 --> 01:18:39.500
I didn't have this
matrix before.

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:42.560
That's an extra matrix multiply
that I have to pay for.

01:18:42.560 --> 01:18:45.370
But the clever thing
here is remember

01:18:45.370 --> 01:18:49.270
that on the other side of k,
where I'm going to take k dot q,

01:18:49.270 --> 01:18:52.270
that q dot k is going to be an
inner product in the attention

01:18:52.270 --> 01:18:52.850
operation.

01:18:52.850 --> 01:18:56.750
And q itself has a
projection matrix Q.

01:18:56.750 --> 01:19:01.000
And so the trick here is you can
merge this WUK and this Q matrix

01:19:01.000 --> 01:19:02.690
together into one matrix.

01:19:02.690 --> 01:19:05.020
I haven't gotten any
extra matrix multiplies.

01:19:05.020 --> 01:19:08.620
I've just merged this new matrix
multiply into my other one.

01:19:08.620 --> 01:19:10.450
This is just associativity.

01:19:10.450 --> 01:19:12.040
I can just merge the two.

01:19:12.040 --> 01:19:14.470
They also compress the
queries for memory savings

01:19:14.470 --> 01:19:15.290
during training.

01:19:15.290 --> 01:19:18.040
But really, that one is
not quite as necessary

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:21.610
because it doesn't interact
at all with the KV cache.

01:19:21.610 --> 01:19:24.550
I'm only going to mention
this last one in passing

01:19:24.550 --> 01:19:26.270
because it is a subtlety.

01:19:26.270 --> 01:19:28.820
But it's kind of a clever
subtlety that you realize,

01:19:28.820 --> 01:19:31.870
which is that this original
trick, this thing that I just

01:19:31.870 --> 01:19:35.350
described at the top, is
not compatible with RoPE.

01:19:35.350 --> 01:19:39.310
And the reason is because the
RoPE matrices-- basically,

01:19:39.310 --> 01:19:41.020
you have the Q's and the K's.

01:19:41.020 --> 01:19:44.230
And you rotate each of those
Q's and the K's by multiplying

01:19:44.230 --> 01:19:46.610
with the rotation
matrix Rq and Rk.

01:19:46.610 --> 01:19:49.450
But if you do that, then
these Rq's and Rk's are

01:19:49.450 --> 01:19:53.500
in between the query projection
and this latent vector

01:19:53.500 --> 01:19:54.800
up projection matrix.

01:19:54.800 --> 01:19:57.250
And since I can't reorder
these matrix multiplies,

01:19:57.250 --> 01:19:59.120
RoPE kind of gets in the way.

01:19:59.120 --> 01:20:03.070
And they still have a solution
of basically doing RoPE

01:20:03.070 --> 01:20:04.760
on non-compressed dimensions.

01:20:04.760 --> 01:20:05.990
That's kind of a side point.

01:20:05.990 --> 01:20:07.490
I think it's not
quite as important.

01:20:07.490 --> 01:20:09.740
You can look at the paper
if you're super interested.

01:20:09.740 --> 01:20:11.800
The other thing that they do--
and this is the last thing,

01:20:11.800 --> 01:20:12.560
I promise--

01:20:12.560 --> 01:20:16.180
is that they have a minor
change in their loss function

01:20:16.180 --> 01:20:20.600
called MTP, where they predict
multiple tokens in parallel.

01:20:20.600 --> 01:20:23.960
And so what they can do is,
normally, you have your inputs.

01:20:23.960 --> 01:20:26.045
You shift them to
the left by one.

01:20:26.045 --> 01:20:27.920
So you're predicting
one token in the future.

01:20:27.920 --> 01:20:29.080
And then your
transformer is going

01:20:29.080 --> 01:20:30.247
to predict all those tokens.

01:20:30.247 --> 01:20:33.140
That's your normal
transformer loss.

01:20:33.140 --> 01:20:35.350
But then what you
can do is, right

01:20:35.350 --> 01:20:36.970
before you make
those predictions,

01:20:36.970 --> 01:20:39.110
you can take the hidden state.

01:20:39.110 --> 01:20:40.990
You can pass it to
a very lightweight

01:20:40.990 --> 01:20:42.650
one-layer transformer.

01:20:42.650 --> 01:20:46.420
And that model can predict
one token in the future.

01:20:46.420 --> 01:20:48.770
So now, the model is not just
predicting the next token.

01:20:48.770 --> 01:20:51.470
It's predicting the two
tokens into the future.

01:20:51.470 --> 01:20:53.640
So that hopefully
all makes sense.

01:20:53.640 --> 01:20:55.390
And this is just a
small lightweight model

01:20:55.390 --> 01:20:57.340
that can do that.

01:20:57.340 --> 01:20:59.890
You can see the
architecture right here.

01:20:59.890 --> 01:21:01.720
The one thing that is
kind of disappointing

01:21:01.720 --> 01:21:03.400
that I learned as
I was researching

01:21:03.400 --> 01:21:04.780
for this lecture
is actually they

01:21:04.780 --> 01:21:06.460
only do MTP with
one token ahead.

01:21:06.460 --> 01:21:08.710
So even though they have
this very complicated diagram

01:21:08.710 --> 01:21:10.990
of how they could do
it for many tokens,

01:21:10.990 --> 01:21:14.390
turns out it's only
done for one token.

01:21:14.390 --> 01:21:16.750
So now, I'm all done.

01:21:16.750 --> 01:21:18.880
MoE's are kind of
now at the core

01:21:18.880 --> 01:21:21.130
of how you would
build and deploy

01:21:21.130 --> 01:21:23.750
a really high-performance,
large-scale system.

01:21:23.750 --> 01:21:26.200
And they take advantage
of this sparsity idea

01:21:26.200 --> 01:21:30.650
that you don't need all of
the parameters all the time.

01:21:30.650 --> 01:21:32.748
And discrete routing is
the real big challenge.

01:21:32.748 --> 01:21:35.290
And this is, I think, one of
the big reasons why MoE's didn't

01:21:35.290 --> 01:21:36.350
immediately catch on.

01:21:36.350 --> 01:21:38.650
It's very scary to
have to try to optimize

01:21:38.650 --> 01:21:40.540
this top-K routing decisions.

01:21:40.540 --> 01:21:42.490
But heuristics
somehow seem to work.

01:21:42.490 --> 01:21:43.760
They just do.

01:21:43.760 --> 01:21:45.730
And so there's a lot
of empirical evidence

01:21:45.730 --> 01:21:49.550
now that MoE's, at least for
flop constrained settings,

01:21:49.550 --> 01:21:50.510
is just a good idea.

01:21:50.510 --> 01:21:51.760
It's cost effective.

01:21:51.760 --> 01:21:52.550
You should do it.

01:21:52.550 --> 01:21:55.030
So definitely worth learning.

01:21:55.030 --> 01:21:58.710
Thanks a lot for listening.
